{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372401941,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_d6a41d8b285a4653856c5fb5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show origin/main:src/parse.rs | sed -n '35,70p'"},"output":"\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Tok {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    Float(f64),\n    Ident(String),\n    Fn,\n    Pub,\n    Type,\nEnum,\n    Let,\n    Input,\n    Inputs,\n    Lines,\n    Dsv,\n    Csv,\n    Tsv,\n    And,\n    Or,\n    Not,\n    Plus,\n    Minus,\n    Star,\n    Slash,\n    Percent,\n    Pipe,\n    PipeGt,\n    Comma,\n    Dot,\n    Eq,\n    EqEq,\n    Ne,\n    Bang,\n    Lt,\n    Le,\n","metadata":{"output":"\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Tok {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    Float(f64),\n    Ident(String),\n    Fn,\n    Pub,\n    Type,\nEnum,\n    Let,\n    Input,\n    Inputs,\n    Lines,\n    Dsv,\n    Csv,\n    Tsv,\n    And,\n    Or,\n    Not,\n    Plus,\n    Minus,\n    Star,\n    Slash,\n    Percent,\n    Pipe,\n    PipeGt,\n    Comma,\n    Dot,\n    Eq,\n    EqEq,\n    Ne,\n    Bang,\n    Lt,\n    Le,\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show origin/main:src/parse.rs | sed -n '35,70p'","time":{"start":1788372401935,"end":1788372401937}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK, so HEAD (our branch, pre-merge) still had If/Else tokens and Stdin (no Let, no Dsv/Csv/Tsv, no Input/Inputs/Lines). \n\nHmm, wait. So the HEAD version of parse.rs is an OLDER version of our branch? Let me check git log on our branch to understand. Maybe our branch is issue-159 which is about something else (the \"retire stdin keywords\" task),and the merge of origin/main brings in Let/Dsv/etc. \n\nActually, let me reconsider: The task per the brief: \"your branch retired the stdin keywords Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv for one Stream<Str> source with parse(.) as a library call\". Hmm, but HEAD parse.rs still has `Stdin` and NOT the retired keywords. Wait -- maybe our branch's work was done in src/parse.rs differently... \n\nHmm, actually maybe our branch changed parse.rs in a way that REMOVED the Input/Inputs/Lines tokens but the git show HEAD I just did shows the enum with `Stdin` and NO Input/Inputs/Lines. So our branch already removed Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv? No wait -- it has `Stdin`, not `Input`. \n\nWait, let me check git log to understand the branch history. The branch might be derived from an old main and the merge into origin/main brings the Input/Inputs/Lines era. That is: origin/main HAS Input/Inputs/Lines tokens (from the grep output above: lines  ǅ47-49 `Input, Inputs, Lines,`), while our branch (HEAD) already retired them and has only `Stdin`.\n\nSo the merge direction: our branch (issue-159) retired Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv; origin/main still has them and has other unrelated changes (Let, conditionals as match arms, Float?, input annotation, etc.).\n\nHmm wait, but origin/main grep showed lines  ǅ47-49 as `Input, Inputs, Lines,` but NOT `Let`. Let me recheck. The origin/main grep output:\n\n```\n47:    Input,\n48:    Inputs,\n49:    Lines,\n50:    Dsv,\n51:    Csv,\n52:    Tsv,\n```\n\nSo origin/main has Input, Inputs, Lines, Dsv, Csv, Tsv but NO Let. And our HEAD has Stdin only (no Input, no Dsv, no Csv, no Tsv, no Let)...\n\nHmm interesting. So our branch at HEAD removed Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv/Csv/Tsv and kept only Stdin. That matches \"retire stdin keywords Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv for one Stream<Str> source\" partially -- but why keep Stdin?\n\nWait, maybe the brief's summary is a paraphrase and the actual branch state is what it is. Our job is just to merge origin/main into our branch, keeping our branch's intent (retiring Input/Inputs/Lines, keeping one stdin source,)and integrating origin/main's unrelated changes.\n\nThe current working-tree parse.rs (as partially resolved by prior runs) has: Enum, Let, Stdin, Dsv, Csv, Tsv tokens... wait, that doesn't match HEAD either. Let me re-read the diff:\n\n```\n enum Tok {\n     Str(String),\n     Int(i64),\n     Ident(String),\n     Fn,\n     Pub,\n     Type,\n-    Enum,\n,\n-    If,\n,\n-    Else,\n+\n+Enum,\n+\n+    Let,\n+\n     Stdin,\n+\n+    Dsv,\n+\n+    Csv,\n+\n+    Tsv,\n+\n     And,\n```\n\nWait, the `+` lines in a git diff for an insertion are the new content. The `-` lines are removed. So the working tree enum has: Str, Int, Ident, Fn, Pub, Type, `Enum,`?? \n\nHmm, the diff shows:\n\n```\n-    Enum,\n-    If,\n-    Else,\n+Enum,\n+    Let,\n     Stdin,\n+    Dsv,\n+    Csv,\n+    Tsv,\n     And,\n```\n\nThis is confusing because of indentation quirks. The context line `    Stdin,` (4 spaces) remains in both. The `+Enum,` line -- wait that's odd. Hmm, maybe git's diff algorithm merged hunks weirdly. Actually I bet the working tree file has:\n\n```\n    Type,\n    Enum,\n    Let,\n    Stdin,\n    Dsv,\n    Csv,\n    Tsv,\n    And,\n```\n\nwith `Enum` at 0 indent? That seems broken. But then earlier I read the working tree file's lines 1312-1314:\n\n```\nmatch tok {\n    Tok::Str(text) => Ok(Expr::Str { text, span }),\n    Tok::Int(value) => Ok(Expr::Int { value, span }),\nTok::Stdin => Ok(Expr::Stdin { span }),\n    Tok::Csv => Ok(Expr::Dsv {\n```\n\nLine  ǅ1314 `Tok::Stdin` at 0 indent -- indeed broken indentation! So the prior agent's partial resolution left formatting broken (line  ǅ1314 has no indent).\n\nOK. This is a mess. The prior runs partially resolved parse.rs with broken formatting and left a dangling marker. \n\nGiven the brief says parse.rs was in the \"8 files still have real conflict markers to resolve with Read+Write\" group, I should fully rewrite parse.rs properly. That is, I need to reconstruct the merged parse.rs carefully.\n\nLet me understand the intended merged state:\n\n- Our branch (HEAD) retired the Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv keywords, leaving Stdin as the only stdin source keyword.\n actually wait, let me look at HEAD's atom function and overall parse.rs to understand the full intended state of our branch.\n- origin/main brought: Let keyword (and associated changes: type defs?, the `input <type>` annotation? no wait -- the annotation uses `input` keyword... hmm.\n\n the diff showed working tree ADDING an \"input <type> declares what stdin holds\" comment block and Let token. But origin/main grep did NOT show `Let` in its token enum (only Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv/Csv/Tsv). Let me check origin/main's token enum fully.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634d3ef70013tfF17cNQwsRUx","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634d14be001hW2MzIexCJZLFn"}}
