g actually reaches it instead of\n148: /// always winning over a parse error earlier in the file the way a separate up-front pass would.\n149: fn read_tok<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n150:     skip_trivia(input);\n151:     let start = input.current_token_start();\n152:     let Some(c) = input.peek_token() else {\n153:         return Ok((Tok::Eof, Span::new(start, start)));\n154:     };\n155: \n\n(Showing lines 36-155 of 1404. Use offset=156 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Tok {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    Ident(String),\n    Fn,\n    Pub,\n    Type,\nEnum,\n    Let,\n    Stdin,\n    Dsv,\n    Csv,\n    Tsv,\n    And,\n    Or,\n    Not,\n    Plus,\n    Minus,\n    Star,","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/parse.rs","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Tok {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    Ident(String),\n    Fn,\n    Pub,\n    Type,\nEnum,\n    Let,\n    Stdin,\n    Dsv,\n    Csv,\n    Tsv,\n    And,\n    Or,\n    Not,\n    Plus,\n    Minus,\n    Star,\n    Slash,\n    Percent,\n    Pipe,\n    Comma,\n    Dot,\n    Eq,\n    EqEq,\n    Ne,\n    Bang,\n    Lt,\n    Le,\n    Gt,\n    Ge,\n    LParen,\n    RParen,\n    LBracket,\n    RBracket,\n    LBrace,\n    RBrace,\n    Colon,\n    Arrow,\n    Eof,\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for Tok {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        let s = match self {\n            Tok::Str(_) => return write!(f, \"a string\"),\n            Tok::Int(n) => return write!(f, \"`{n}`\"),\n            Tok::Ident(name) => return write!(f, \"`{name}`\"),\n            Tok::Fn => \"`fn`\",\n            Tok::Pub => \"`pub`\",\n            Tok::Type => \"`type`\",\n            Tok::Enum => \"`enum`\",\nTok::Let => \"`let`\",\n            Tok::Stdin => \"`stdin`\",\n            Tok::Dsv => \"`dsv`\",\n            Tok::Csv => \"`csv`\",\n            Tok::Tsv => \"`tsv`\",\n            Tok::And => \"`and`\",\n            Tok::Or => \"`or`\",\n            Tok::Not => \"`not`\",\n            Tok::Plus => \"`+`\",\n            Tok::Minus => \"`-`\",\n            Tok::Star => \"`*`\",\n            Tok::Slash => \"`/`\",\n            Tok::Percent => \"`%`\",\n            Tok::Pipe => \"`|`\",\n            Tok::Comma => \"`,`\",\n            Tok::Dot => \"`.`\",\n            Tok::Eq => \"`=`\",\n            Tok::EqEq => \"`==`\",\n            Tok::Ne => \"`!=`\",\n            Tok::Bang => \"`!`\",\n            Tok::Lt => \"`<`\",\n            Tok::Le => \"`<=`\",\n            Tok::Gt => \"`>`\",\n            Tok::Ge => \"`>=`\",\n            Tok::LParen => \"`(`\",\n            Tok::RParen => \"`)`\",\n            Tok::LBracket => \"`[`\",\n            Tok::RBracket => \"`]`\",\n            Tok::LBrace => \"`{`\",\n            Tok::RBrace => \"`}`\",\n            Tok::Colon => \"`:`\",\n            Tok::Arrow => \"`->`\",\n            Tok::Eof => \"end of program\",\n        };\n        write!(f, \"{s}\")\n    }\n}\n\n/// Zero or more ASCII spaces/tabs/newlines, then a `#`-to-newline comment if one follows,\n/// repeated: a run of trivia can mix any number of either in any order.\nfn skip_trivia(input: &mut Input) {\n    loop {\n        take_while::<_, _, Error>(0.., |c: char| c.is_ascii_whitespace())\n            .parse_next(input)\n            .expect(\"a lower bound of zero never fails\");\n        if input.peek_token() != Some('#') {\n            return;\n        }\n        input.next_token();\n        while !matches!(input.peek_token(), None | Some('\\n')) {\n            input.next_token();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Skips trivia, then reads exactly one token from the front of `input`. Called fresh for every\n/// `peek`/`peek2`/`advance` rather than once up front into a `Vec`, so a lexical error (a bad\n/// escape, an out-of-range integer) surfaces at the point parsing actually reaches it instead of\n/// always winning over a parse error earlier in the file the way a separate up-front pass would.\nfn read_tok<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n    skip_trivia(input);\n    let start = input.current_token_start();\n    let Some(c) = input.peek_token() else {\n        return Ok((Tok::Eof, Span::new(start, start)));\n    };\n","lineStart":36,"lineEnd":155,"totalLines":1404,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/parse.rs","time":{"start":1788372422335,"end":1788372422342}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK, now I understand the two sides better.\n\n**HEAD (our branch)** parse.rs:\n- No Float token\n- No Let token\n- No If/Else tokens (already removed? Or never had? It has If/Else per earlier grep -- wait, the enum at HEAD had `Enum, If, Else, Stdin,`. And the display had `Tok::If => \"`if`\"`. Hmm but no \"if\"/\"else\" parsing? Let me check. Actually HEAD has If/Else tokens.)\n\nWait, this is getting complicated. Let me look at the HEAD atom function above: it handles Tok::Stdin, and I saw earlier the HEAD grep for Stdin showed line  ǅ1209 `Tok::Stdin => Ok(Expr::Stdin { span }),`. It does NOT handle Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv/Csv/Tsv. So HEAD has no Dsv/Csv/Tsv tokens. But it has If/Else tokens? Let me verify HEAD's enum once more:\n\nFrom `git show HEAD:src/parse.rs | sed -n '35,110p'`:\n```\n    Enum,\n    If,\n    Else,\n    Stdin,\n    And,...\n```\nYes, HEAD has If/Else tokens. And its Display has If/Else. Does the parser actually parse \"if\"/\"else\"? Let me search HEAD for \"Tok::If\".\n\nActually wait -- maybe HEAD's If/Else tokens are vestigial (used only in error messages or the conditional is now `a if c else b` via a different mechanism?. The comment in the diff mentioned \"The conditional sits between `|` and the Bool connectives\" -- so HEAD has a conditional `a if c else b` syntax? And origin/main dropped If/Else in favor of match arms? Hmm.\n\nActually the diff hunk showed:\n\n```\n-/// The conditional sits between `|` and the Bool connectives, so `a if c else b | f` groups as\n...\n+/// Where a match arm's body and its guard operand are read and printed: between `|` (1/2)and\n```\n\nSo HEAD (the `-` lines) had the conditional `a if c else b`; origin/main (the `+` lines) replaced it with match arms (`a if ... -> ... |`? with guards). So origin/main dropped If/Else tokens (replaced conditional with match). Wait but origin/main's enum has NO If/Else (from the full enum listing above: no If/Else). Correct: origin/main removed If/Else entirely. And HEAD still HAS If/Else tokens (it has the conditional syntax).\n\nHmm wait, but that contradicts: HEAD = commit 45c76be \"Retire input/inputs/lines...\" which is our branch's top. The diff hunk `-` lines are from HEAD? Let me re-examine. `git diff HEAD -- src/parse.rs` -- the `-` lines are HEAD's content, the `+` lines are working-tree content.\n\n- HEAD had: \"The conditional sits between `|` and the Bool connectives, so `a if c else b | f` groups as `(a if c else b) | f`...\"\n+ working tree has: \"Where a match arm's body and its guard operand are read and printed...\"\n\nHmm, so HEAD had the conditional `a if c else b` docs, and the working tree (prior agent's resolution) replaced it with match-arm docs (which is what origin/main has...\n\nWait, hold on. That doesn't make sense directionally. If HEAD had the conditional and origin/main has match arms, then merging origin/main into HEAD should keep... our branch's content? Actually the merge resolution should pick either side or integrate both. \n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider which branch is \"ahead\". The merge is `git merge origin/main` performed on branch issue-159. Our branch is issue-159. HEAD = issue-159's tip = 45c76be (Retire input/inputs/lines...). origin/main is the other side being merged in.\n\nThe conflict is between HEAD's version of parse.rs and origin/main's version.\n\n.\n\nHEAD's parse.rs: has If/Else tokens (conditional syntax), no Float, no Let, no Dsv/Csv/Tsv, no Input/Inputs/Lines, has Stdin.\n\n. Origin/main's parse.rs: has Float, Let, Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv/Csv/Tsv, no If/Else, has PipeGt (pipe |>?). Hmm wait, PipeGt also.\n\n Let me check if HEAD has PipeGt.\n\n From HEAD enum above: `Slash, Percent, Pipe, Comma,...` no PipeGt. So origin/main added PipeGt too.\n\n and Float, Let, and it still has Input/Inputs/Lines (pre-retirement), and Dsv/Csv/Tsv, and no If/Else (replaced conditional with match).\n\nHmm wait, actually maybe origin/main ALSO has If/Else? The enum listing of origin/main I printed (lines 38-90) shows: `...Type, Enum, Let, Input, Inputs, Lines, Dsv, Csv, Tsv, And,...` -- NO If/Else, NO Stdin. So origin/main has NO Stdin! It has Input/Inputs/Lines instead. And HEAD has Stdin but no Input/Inputs/Lines.\n\n\n\nSo the two sides represent DIFFERENT lineages:\n- HEAD: post-retirement: only `stdin` keyword (source), no Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv/Csv/Tsv, has If/Else conditional, no Let, no Float, no PipeGt.\n.\n - origin/main: pre-retirement: Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv/Csv/Tsv keywords, no stdin, no If/Else (match replaced it, has Let, Float, PipeGt.\n\nHmm wait, but this is weird: origin/main is \"ahead\" of HEAD in many ways (Let, Float, PipeGt, match arms) but \"behind\" on the retirement. Actually, the repo is a set of lanes where different issues merge into main in some order. The brief says \"your branch retired the stdin keywords Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv for one Stream<Str> source with parse(.) as a library call\". \n\nHmm, wait: \"for one Stream<Str> source\" -- maybe the retirement means: instead of multiple source keywords (input, inputs, lines, dsv,, the source is just `stdin` and it holds a Stream<Str>? But then why does HEAD still have If/Else and no Let? Because HEAD's parse.rs predates the Let/match-arm work (which landed in main via another lane),and predates Float/PipeGt.\n\n.\n\n\n\nOK so the merge goal: take our branch's retirement intent (stdin as the one source keyword) BUT also integrate origin/main's unrelated changes (Let, Float, PipeGt, match arms replacing if/else...). \n\nWait, but hold on: is \"match arms replacing if/else\" actually origin/main's change or ours? The brief's AGENTS.md mentions `selection.narrow` and docs/reference/operators/match.md modified. And the conflict is intricate. \n\nHmm, actually I realize I should be more careful. The two branches diverged from some common ancestor. Our branch (issue-159) is an older lane that made the retirement change. origin/main is newer and has accumulated Let/Float/PipeGt/match-arms etc. Since our branch's whole purpose (gh:159) is retiring stdin keywords, merging origin/main into our branch means: adopt all origin/main's changes EXCEPT where they conflict with our retirement intent. Where origin/main still has Input/Inputs/Lines keyword machinery, our branch removed it -- so in conflicts, we keep our branch's removal.\n\n. But for unrelated changes (Let, Float, PipeGt, match arms, etc.), we adopt origin/main's version.\n\n.\n\n\n\nHmm, but wait: is match-arms/if-else related to our task? The brief said \"keep that intent, integrate whatever unrelated changes origin/main made to the same file\". So If/Else vs match is unrelated to stdin keywords. So we should adopt origin/main's match-arms version (dropping If/Else).. \n\nBut wait -- hmm, actually is that right? Let me reconsider. Our branch HEAD has If/Else (the conditional `a if c else b`). origin/main has no If/Else (uses match arms with guards instead`. This is an \"unrelated change\" that landed in main. So merged result should adopt origin/main's version: no If/Else, match arms.\n\n.But actually -- hold on. Let me double check that our branch's HEAD truly uses If/Else and origin/main truly uses match arms. Let me look at HEAD's parse.rs around the COND_POWER comment and infix parsing, and origin/main's equivalent.\n\n.\n\nActually, I realize I'm going deep. Let me instead step back and consider the pragmatic approach:\n\nThe prior runs already did most of the conflict resolution across the 12 files. Only 11 files still have markers, and src/parse.rs has just a dangling `>>>>>>> origin/main` at line  ǅ1341. The working tree content for parse.rs (from the diff vs HEAD) looks like a coherent merge already: it has Let, Dsv, Csv, Tsv, Stdin (no Input/Inputs/Lines, no If/Else... wait, does it have If/Else?.\n\n \n\nFrom the diff hunk at lines around enum:\n\n```\n-    Enum,\n-    If,\n-    Else,\n+Enum,\n+    Let,\n     Stdin,\n+    Dsv,\n+    Csv,\n+    Tsv,\n```\n\nHmm, the `-` removed lines are `Enum, If, Else,`(from HEAD)and the `+` added lines are `Enum, Let,`... wait, this hunk is really confusing because `Enum,` appears in both - and +. Let me get the actual working-tree enum lines directly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634d8eb6001fQ5n29fw6uIVmG","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634d5041001Vz0A7rDp2z06sY"}}
