tadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK, now I have a clear picture. The prior agent's \"resolution\" of parse.rs was basically: keep HEAD's version (our branch) BUT add Let/Dsv/Csv/Tsv tokens from origin/main. It did NOT adopt origin/main's Float, PipeGt, two-char token reading, tail_pipe removal, or match-arm changes...\n\nWait, actually let me re-read: the working tree has `let body = p.expr(0)?;` (no tail_pipe) and removed the `tail_pipe` function? From the diff vs origin/main: `-` lines are origin/main content, `+` lines are working tree. In the hunk at 410:\n\n```\n-    let body = p.tail_pipe()?;\n+    let body = p.expr(0)?;\n```\n\nSo origin/main calls `p.tail_pipe()`; working tree calls `p.expr(0)`. So the working tree REMOVED tail_pipe (the `|>` sink syntax)which is an origin/main feature... wait, no. Hmm.\n\nHold on. `git diff origin/main -- src/parse.rs` shows changes FROM origin/main TO working tree. So:\n- Origin/main HAS `tail_pipe` (and `PipeGt` token);\n- Working tree does NOT have tail_pipe (reverted to expr(0)).\n\nBut wait -- earlier the diff vs HEAD showed:\n\n```\n+/// Where a match arm's body...\n```\nas `+` working tree content... vs HEAD had \"The conditional sits between `|`...\"\n\nHmm, so vs HEAD, working tree ADDED the match-arm comment (which is origin/main's),and vs origin/main, working tree REMOVED tail_pipe, Float, PipeGt, read_two_char... \n\nSo the working tree is a weird hybrid: \n- from HEAD: Stdin (retirement), no Input/Inputs/Lines, no Float, no PipeGt, no tail_pipe, `p.expr(0)` \n- from origin/main: Let, Dsv/Csv/Tsv tokens, match-arm comment, `or` separator...\n- broken formatting everywhere, and a dangling `>>>>>>> origin/main` marker.\n\n \n\nThis is clearly NOT a faithful merge of the two. It's the prior agent's botched partial work. I should reconstruct parse.rs properly.\n\n\n\nOK so now the big question: what is the CORRECT merged parse.rs? I need to decide the merge policy:\n\n- The intent of our branch (gh:159): retire the stdin keywords `input`, `inputs`, `lines` (and `dsv`'s keyword position?) for one `stdin` source... Actually re-reading the brief: \"your branch retired the stdin keywords Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv for one Stream<Str> sourcewith parse(.) as a library call -- keep that intent\".\n\nSo our branch: removed `input`, `inputs`, `lines`, and `dsv`(as keyword? Wait Dsv is a delimiter-parameterized source, in origin/main there's `dsv(\"...\")` form AND bare `csv`/`tsv`... Hmm.\n\n\n\nLet me look at HEAD's parse.rs full to understand what \"one Stream<Str> source\" means. Let me look at HEAD's src/parse.rs the whole file (it's ~1260 lines). Let me also look at what changed between HEAD and origin/main for the relevant parts.\n\n\n\nActually, maybe a cleaner path: resolve parse.rs by taking origin/main's version and applying our branch's retirement changes onto it. That is: origin/main is strictly newer for everything except the retirement. Our branch's retirement = remove Input/Inputs/Lines tokens (and keep Stdin? no, wait-- origin/main has no Stdin; it uses Input/Inputs/Lines/...).\n\nHmm wait, actually, I need to look at what our branch did MORE carefully. HEAD's parse.rs uses `Tok::Stdin` and `Expr::Stdin`. Origin/main uses `Tok::Input`, `Tok::Inputs`, `Tok::Lines` and `Expr::Input`, `Expr::Inputs`, `Expr::Lines`. So our branch RENAMED Input/Inputs/Lines→ Stdin? That seems odd for \"retire ... for one Stream<Str> source\". \n\nActually maybe the story: the language previously had multiple stdin sources:`input` (whole input), `inputs` (split by lines?), `lines`, `dsv`, `csv`, `tsv`. Our branch replaced the multi-source system with ONE `stdin` source. And \"parse(.) as a library call\" = the `dsv(\"...\")` / csv parsing is now done via a `parse` function/library call instead of a keyword. Let me verify: HEAD has `Expr::Stdin` (line 1209) and no `Expr::Dsv`? Let me grep HEAD for Expr::Dsv and parse(.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634dd5f5001JPyvi13G95X6sJ","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634dae6c001u5KMD2SmMDfBv6"}}
