{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372445805,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_3df5e0cfc8c54a298be2b21d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show --stat 45c76be"},"output":"commit 45c76bedb230aa116e19dbd1e30e47aee4ca4d94\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Tue Sep 1 23:26:58 2026 +0200\n\n    Retire input/inputs/lines for one stdin source with parse as a library call\n    \n    The stdin-syntax-design ruling (gh:159, tentative): three keywords become a\n    single raw Stream<Str> source named `stdin`, and reading a value is an\n    ordinary `parse(.)` call over it. The parser, checker, TIR, and every backend\n    now carry `stdin` and the `parse` builtin instead of the three source forms;\n    the host connects live stdin only when the program reads the source.\n    \n    Source migration only; the corpus and test files still spell the old keywords\n    and land in a follow-up.\n    \n    Human-authored: the single-source shape and parse-as-library-call semantics\n    Derived: per-backend parse emission from each target's native JSON handling\n    Agent-invented: the parse_read checked-form wiring in the checker\n    \n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode <git@daniel-kantor.com>\n\n build.rs               |   5 +-\n runtime/toylang.c      | 149 +++--------------------\n src/ast.rs             |  21 +---\n src/check/linearity.rs |  10 +-\n src/check/mod.rs       | 254 +++++++++++----------------------------\n src/emit_go.rs         | 127 ++++++++++----------\n src/emit_jq.rs         |  32 ++---\n src/emit_js.rs         |  43 ++-----\n src/emit_llvm.rs       | 114 ++++--------------\n src/emit_lua.rs        | 137 ++++++++++++++++------\n src/emit_py.rs         |  40 ++-----\n src/emit_rs.rs         | 120 ++++++-------------\n src/emit_toylang.rs    |   4 +-\n src/lib.rs             | 313 +++++++++----------------------------------------\n src/main.rs            |  24 +---\n src/parse.rs           |  18 +--\n src/tags.rs            |   9 +-\n src/tir.rs             |  43 +++----\n 18 files changed, 436 insertions(+), 1027 deletions(-)\n","metadata":{"output":"commit 45c76bedb230aa116e19dbd1e30e47aee4ca4d94\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Tue Sep 1 23:26:58 2026 +0200\n\n    Retire input/inputs/lines for one stdin source with parse as a library call\n    \n    The stdin-syntax-design ruling (gh:159, tentative): three keywords become a\n    single raw Stream<Str> source named `stdin`, and reading a value is an\n    ordinary `parse(.)` call over it. The parser, checker, TIR, and every backend\n    now carry `stdin` and the `parse` builtin instead of the three source forms;\n    the host connects live stdin only when the program reads the source.\n    \n    Source migration only; the corpus and test files still spell the old keywords\n    and land in a follow-up.\n    \n    Human-authored: the single-source shape and parse-as-library-call semantics\n    Derived: per-backend parse emission from each target's native JSON handling\n    Agent-invented: the parse_read checked-form wiring in the checker\n    \n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode <git@daniel-kantor.com>\n\n build.rs               |   5 +-\n runtime/toylang.c      | 149 +++--------------------\n src/ast.rs             |  21 +---\n src/check/linearity.rs |  10 +-\n src/check/mod.rs       | 254 +++++++++++----------------------------\n src/emit_go.rs         | 127 ++++++++++----------\n src/emit_jq.rs         |  32 ++---\n src/emit_js.rs         |  43 ++-----\n src/emit_llvm.rs       | 114 ++++--------------\n src/emit_lua.rs        | 137 ++++++++++++++++------\n src/emit_py.rs         |  40 ++-----\n src/emit_rs.rs         | 120 ++++++-------------\n src/emit_toylang.rs    |   4 +-\n src/lib.rs             | 313 +++++++++----------------------------------------\n src/main.rs            |  24 +---\n src/parse.rs           |  18 +--\n src/tags.rs            |   9 +-\n src/tir.rs             |  43 +++----\n 18 files changed, 436 insertions(+), 1027 deletions(-)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show --stat 45c76be","time":{"start":1788372445802,"end":1788372445804}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The merge base is 7c4cc86 = \"Accept snapshots after merging issue-140\". And HEAD's parent is also 7c4cc86? HEAD's log: `45c76be Retire input/inputs/lines...` then `7c4cc86 Accept snapshots after merging issue-140`. So HEAD is exactly ONE commit ahead of the merge base: our branch is just 45c76be.\n\nSo the merge: merge-base = 7c4cc86; ours (HEAD) = 7c4cc86 + 45c76be (retirement); theirs (origin/main) = 7c4cc86 + MANY commits (all the main-line work since: Float, Let, match arms, PipeGt/tail_pipe, Dsv/Csv/Tsv, Input/Inputs/Lines... etc).\n\nSo origin/main made all these changes (Let, Float, PipeGt, dsv sources etc.) AFTER the merge base, and our branch made exactly one change (retire stdin keywords)..\n\n \n\nThe correct merge: apply our one-commit retirement on top of origin/main. That is: take origin/main's version of each file, and apply our branch's retirement change. Since our branch's only change vs the base is the retirement, and origin/main diverged heavily, most of the conflict resolution should = take origin/main's content and re-apply the retirement.\n\n.\n\nLet me see what our one commit 45c76be changed.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634dea600018d3dzLErqz46vg","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634dde3a001PJX5MtuVqT6bPG"}}
