{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372451026,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_88f77cee2307465facbbd292","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show 45c76be -- src/parse.rs"},"output":"commit 45c76bedb230aa116e19dbd1e30e47aee4ca4d94\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Tue Sep 1 23:26:58 2026 +0200\n\n    Retire input/inputs/lines for one stdin source with parse as a library call\n    \n    The stdin-syntax-design ruling (gh:159, tentative): three keywords become a\n    single raw Stream<Str> source named `stdin`, and reading a value is an\n    ordinary `parse(.)` call over it. The parser, checker, TIR, and every backend\n    now carry `stdin` and the `parse` builtin instead of the three source forms;\n    the host connects live stdin only when the program reads the source.\n    \n    Source migration only; the corpus and test files still spell the old keywords\n    and land in a follow-up.\n    \n    Human-authored: the single-source shape and parse-as-library-call semantics\n    Derived: per-backend parse emission from each target's native JSON handling\n    Agent-invented: the parse_read checked-form wiring in the checker\n    \n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode <git@daniel-kantor.com>\n\ndiff --git a/src/parse.rs b/src/parse.rs\nindex 69ccc21..d54a7eb 100644\n--- a/src/parse.rs\n+++ b/src/parse.rs\n@@ -44,9 +44,7 @@ enum Tok {\n     Enum,\n     If,\n     Else,\n-    Input,\n-    Inputs,\n-    Lines,\n+    Stdin,\n     And,\n     Or,\n     Not,\n@@ -89,9 +87,7 @@ impl std::fmt::Display for Tok {\n             Tok::Enum => \"`enum`\",\n             Tok::If => \"`if`\",\n             Tok::Else => \"`else`\",\n-            Tok::Input => \"`input`\",\n-            Tok::Inputs => \"`inputs`\",\n-            Tok::Lines => \"`lines`\",\n+            Tok::Stdin => \"`stdin`\",\n             Tok::And => \"`and`\",\n             Tok::Or => \"`or`\",\n             Tok::Not => \"`not`\",\n@@ -179,9 +175,7 @@ fn read_tok<'i>(input: &mut Input<'i>) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n                 \"enum\" => Tok::Enum,\n                 \"if\" => Tok::If,\n                 \"else\" => Tok::Else,\n-                \"input\" => Tok::Input,\n-                \"inputs\" => Tok::Inputs,\n-                \"lines\" => Tok::Lines,\n+                \"stdin\" => Tok::Stdin,\n                 \"and\" => Tok::And,\n                 // Two operators sharing one spelling, told apart by position: the arm separator\n                 // at a chain's top level, Bool disjunction everywhere else. `Cursor::or_separates`\n@@ -1165,7 +1159,7 @@ impl<'i> Cursor<'i> {\n         }\n         let argument_starts = match next {\n             Tok::LParen | Tok::LBrace => true,\n-            Tok::Str(_) | Tok::Int(_) | Tok::Input | Tok::Inputs | Tok::Lines | Tok::Ident(_) => {\n+            Tok::Str(_) | Tok::Int(_) | Tok::Stdin | Tok::Ident(_) => {\n                 bare_callee(&name)\n             }\n             _ => false,\n@@ -1212,9 +1206,7 @@ impl<'i> Cursor<'i> {\n         match tok {\n             Tok::Str(text) => Ok(Expr::Str { text, span }),\n             Tok::Int(value) => Ok(Expr::Int { value, span }),\n-            Tok::Input => Ok(Expr::Input { span }),\n-            Tok::Inputs => Ok(Expr::Inputs { span }),\n-            Tok::Lines => Ok(Expr::Lines { span }),\n+            Tok::Stdin => Ok(Expr::Stdin { span }),\n \n             // `.name` is field access on the subject, so the leading dot yields `.` and the\n             // postfix loop above picks the field up.\n","metadata":{"output":"commit 45c76bedb230aa116e19dbd1e30e47aee4ca4d94\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Tue Sep 1 23:26:58 2026 +0200\n\n    Retire input/inputs/lines for one stdin source with parse as a library call\n    \n    The stdin-syntax-design ruling (gh:159, tentative): three keywords become