ain\nsrc/tir.rs:278:<<<<<<< HEAD\nsrc/tir.rs:282:=======\nsrc/tir.rs:294:>>>>>>> origin/main\nsrc/tir.rs:331:<<<<<<< HEAD\nsrc/tir.rs:337:=======\nsrc/tir.rs:346:>>>>>>> origin/main\nsrc/tir.rs:361:<<<<<<< HEAD\nsrc/tir.rs:363:=======\nsrc/tir.rs:367:>>>>>>> origin/main\nsrc/tir.rs:382:<<<<<<< HEAD\nsrc/tir.rs:384:=======\nsrc/tir.rs:393:>>>>>>> origin/main\nsrc/tir.rs:471:<<<<<<< HEAD\nsrc/tir.rs:473:=======\nsrc/tir.rs:477:>>>>>>> origin/main\nsrc/tir.rs:562:<<<<<<< HEAD\nsrc/tir.rs:564:=======\nsrc/tir.rs:569:>>>>>>> origin/main\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git grep -n -E '^(<<<<<<<|=======|>>>>>>>)' -- 'src/check/mod.rs' 'src/emit_go.rs' 'src/emit_jq.rs' 'src/emit_js.rs' 'src/emit_llvm.rs' 'src/emit_lua.rs' 'src/emit_py.rs' 'src/emit_rs.rs' 'src/emit_toylang.rs' 'src/lib.rs' 'src/tir.rs' 2>/dev/null","time":{"start":1788372469306,"end":1788372469311}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a large commit. Rather than trying to reproduce every detail manually, I should take a smarter approach.\n\nThe merge situation:\n- Base = 7c4cc86\n- Ours = base + 45c76be (retirement)\n- Theirs = origin/main (many commits later)\n\nThe CORRECT merge = apply 45c76be's changes onto origin/main's tree.\n\nSince git already did a merge and left conflicts, for each conflicted file I can:\n1. Get origin/main's version of the file.\n2. Get our version of the file.\n3. Determine our branch's DELTA from the base (that's exactly what 45c76be did to the base version).\n4. Apply that same delta to origin/main's version.\n\nBut actually, this is what git's \"merge\" does automatically when there's no conflict. For conflicted files, I manually do it.\n\n.\n\nHowever, there's a subtlety: our delta touches regions that origin/main heavily rewrote. E.g. in src/lib.rs, origin/main might have changed the Input/Inputs/Lines code extensively (or moved it).. I need to look at each file.\n\n.\n\nThe most reliable approach given the tools: for each of the 11 files with markers, produce the merged file by hand (Read+Write) based on understanding both sides. But that's enormous for 11 files (lib.rs is 300+ lines of changes...).\n\nAlternative smarter approach: Use git's \"merge-file\" or \"git merge -X ours/theirs\"? No, can't.\n\n.\n\nActually, here's a cleaner idea: Since our delta is exactly one commit, I can compute our delta against the base for each file, and then apply it onto origin/main's file content using the patch tools. But the brief prohibits shell loop constructs, heredocs, redirections... and direct binary execution. Patching via `git apply` might be denied too. Hmm.\n\n The brief says \"CREATE AND EDIT FILES ONLY WITH YOUR write/edit TOOLS, one simple command per bash call.\"\n\nSo I must use write/edit tools to produce files. OK.\n\n \n\nGiven the scale, maybe the right approach is to focus on the CONFLICT REGIONS only. Git already merged the non-conflicting parts of each file (the working tree contains those merges already, with conflict markers around the conflicted hunks.). So for each file, I need to:\n\n1. Read the file.\n2. For each conflict marker block, decide the resolution (origin/main's content, adjusted for our retirement delta if that region is affected).\n3. Remove markers and produce the final file via Write.\n\n\n\nThe prior agent already resolved most files (4 claimed fully resolved, plus possibly more partially).. The remaining 11 files have markers. For each, the bulk of the file is already merged. I just need to resolve the marked regions.\n\n..\n\nGiven the number of files, let me approach systematically. Let me look at each conflicted file's markers first, one at a time. Let me start with the smaller/easier ones: src/tir.rs, src/emit_toylang.rs, src/emit_jq.rs, src/emit_js.rs, src/emit_lua.rs, src/emit_py.rs, src/emit_go.rs, src/emit_llvm.rs, src/emit_rs.rs, src/check/mod.rs, src/lib.rs.\n\n\n\nLet me first check the conflict marker locations in each file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634e46230019oWpftPtcY8hVi","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634e252a0010xAmPR445fCEKq"}}
