{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372471848,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_9c705ed2a6c94d49a6ae8a03","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs","limit":120,"offset":1},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The typed IR the backends consume.\n2: //!\n3: //! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n4: //! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n5: //! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n6: //!\n7: //! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n8: //! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n9: \n10: use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\n11: use crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n12: \n13: pub struct Tir {\n14:     pub ty: Type,\n15:     pub kind: Kind,\n16: }\n17: \n18: impl Tir {\n19:     pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n20:         Tir { ty, kind }\n21:     }\n22: }\n23: \n24: /// A binding the source cannot name, introduced for `|` and for `select`'s parameter.\n25: pub type LocalId = u32;\n26: \n27: pub enum Kind {\n28:     Str(String),\n29:     Int(i64),\n30:     VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n31:     /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n32:     /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n33:     /// searching for it.\n34:     RecordLit {\n35:         fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n36:     },\n37:     /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n38:     /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n39:     /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n40:     /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n41:     /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n42:     EnumLit {\n43:         variant: String,\n44:         payload: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n45:     },\n46:     /// Rebuilds an Opt: present-preserving, absent-preserving. `body` runs with `param` bound\n47:     /// to the unwrapped payload only when `source` is present; an absent `source` passes\n48:     /// through untouched. Never surface syntax -- Opt has no general map or match a program can\n49:     /// reach (kantord/toylang#47's totality round owns that) -- this is only how the checker's\n50:     /// own reorder pass reaches inside an Opt payload (kantord/toylang#66): every other enum\n51:     /// reorders through `Match`/`EnumLit`, but Opt keeps a representation of its own in three\n52:     /// backends, so those two general nodes cannot reach it.\n53:     OptMap {\n54:         source: Box<Tir>,\n55:         param: LocalId,\n56:         body: Box<Tir>,\n57:     },\n58:     /// A name written in the source: today only a function parameter.\n59:     Var(String),\n60:     Local(LocalId),\n61: <<<<<<< HEAD\n62:     /// The one source: the stream of raw lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>`.\n63:     Stdin,\n64: =======\n65:     /// The value read from stdin.\n66:     Input,\n67:     /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, eagerly collected into a `Vec<T>`.\n68:     Inputs,\n69:     /// The stream of lines read from stdin, read incrementally by whatever consumes it.\n70:     Lines,\n71:     /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`: the\n72:     /// parameterized DSV source. `csv`/`tsv` arrive here with the delimiter already fixed.\n73:     Dsv {\n74:         delim: String,\n75:     },\n76: >>>>>>> origin/main\n77:     Call {\n78:         func: String,\n79:         /// `None` for a call to a nullary function.\n80:         arg: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n81:     },\n82:     Concat(Box<Tir>, Box<Tir>),\n83:     /// Wrapping integer arithmetic, at the width the node's own type names: 32 bits for `Int`,\n84:     /// 64 for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83) -- both operands always share it, since nothing\n85:     /// converts implicitly. Division and remainder stop the program on a zero divisor, which\n86:     /// is the only way arithmetic can fail.\n87:     Arith {\n88:         op: BinOp,\n89:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n90:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n91:     },\n92:     Compare {\n93:         op: BinOp,\n94:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n95:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n96:     },\n97:     /// `and` / `or` over two Bools, short-circuiting: `rhs` is evaluated only when `lhs` leaves\n98:     /// the answer open. That is not a nicety here -- division and `!` can stop the program, so\n99:     /// whether the right side runs is observable -- which is why every backend emits its own\n100:     /// short-circuiting operator rather than two evaluated operands combined afterwards.\n101:     Logic {\n102:         op: LogicOp,\n103:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n104:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n105:     },\n106:     /// `not b`.\n107:     Not(Box<Tir>),\n108:     /// `let local = value in body`, which is what `|` becomes once `.` has a name.\n109:     Bind {\n110:         local: LocalId,\n111:         value: Box<Tir>,\n112:         body: Box<Tir>,\n113:     },\n114:     /// Every element replaced by `body`, with `param` bound to each. Same loop as Select, kept\n115:     /// separate because the result's element type is the body's rather than the source's.\n116:     Map {\n117:         source: Box<Tir>,\n118:         param: LocalId,\n119:         body: Box<Tir>,\n120:     },\n\n(Showing lines 1-120 of 646. Use offset=121 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The typed IR the backends consume.\n//!\n//! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n//! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n//! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n//!\n//! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n//! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n\npub struct Tir {\n    pub ty: Type,\n    pub kind: Kind,\n}\n\nimpl Tir {\n    pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n        Tir { ty, kind }","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs","text":"//! The typed IR the backends consume.\n//!\n//! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n//! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n//! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n//!\n//! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n//! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n\npub struct Tir {\n    pub ty: Type,\n    pub kind: Kind,\n}\n\nimpl Tir {\n    pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n        Tir { ty, kind }\n    }\n}\n\n/// A binding the source cannot name, introduced for `|` and for `select`'s parameter.\npub type LocalId = u32;\n\npub enum Kind {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n    /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n    /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n    /// searching for it.\n    RecordLit {\n        fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n    },\n    /// A constructed enum value. The node's