{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372476178,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_6d2d9eb7aa7941afa1697ab4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs","limit":160,"offset":260},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n260:     /// `parse(s)`, `Str -> T`: parse one line of stdin (a raw `Str`) as a value of the type\n261:     /// the position expects. The one place a wire value enters the program, so it is checked\n262:     /// against the wanted type the way a source used to be, and a backend emits it as its own\n263:     /// per-type JSON parser over the argument string.\n264:     Parse,\n265: }\n266: \n267: pub struct Func {\n268:     pub name: String,\n269:     /// `None` for a nullary function.\n270:     pub param: Option<String>,\n271:     pub param_ty: Option<Type>,\n272:     pub body: Tir,\n273: }\n274: \n275: pub struct Program {\n276:     pub funcs: Vec<Func>,\n277:     pub body: Tir,\n278: <<<<<<< HEAD\n279:     /// Whether the program reads `stdin`. A stream is read at most once, and the flag is what\n280:     /// lets the host connect the real stdin through rather than pre-reading it.\n281:     pub uses_stdin: bool,\n282: =======\n283:     /// The type stdin must have, if the program reads it.\n284:     pub input: Option<Type>,\n285:     /// The element type each line of stdin parses as, if the program reads `inputs`.\n286:     pub inputs: Option<Type>,\n287:     /// Whether the program reads `lines`. A separate flag from `input`, since the two are\n288:     /// unrelated readers of the same real stdin and a program using `lines` alone still needs\n289:     /// it connected, even though `input` is `None`.\n290:     pub uses_lines: bool,\n291:     /// The delimiter the program splits raw lines on, if it reads `dsv`/`csv`/`tsv`. It\n292:     /// reads the same real stdin as the other three, so it is exclusive with them.\n293:     pub dsv: Option<String>,\n294: >>>>>>> origin/main\n295:     /// Every enum the program declared, the prelude's included. A backend has no checker `Ctx`\n296:     /// to hand, and the variant list on a `Type::Enum` is a placeholder wherever a recursive\n297:     /// enum's payload reaches back to itself, so this travels with the tree: it is what\n298:     /// `ty::variants` re-derives from (kantord/toylang#94).\n299:     pub enums: Enums,\n300: }\n301: \n302: /// The element type a backend iterates over. Under eager lowering a stream is materialized as\n303: /// the Vec of its entries, so a Stream's element counts exactly as a Vec's here; the checker's\n304: /// `Type::elem` deliberately does not agree, keeping the reducers Vec-only at the surface.\n305: pub fn runtime_elem(ty: &Type) -> Option<&Type> {\n306:     match ty {\n307:         Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some(t),\n308:         _ => None,\n309:     }\n310: }\n311: \n312: /// How many dimension layers wrap a scalar -- `Vec`s, plus the outermost `Stream` when there\n313: /// is one. Field access distributes over each of them.\n314: pub fn vec_depth(ty: &Type) -> usize {\n315:     let mut depth = 0;\n316:     let mut inner = ty;\n317:     while let Some(elem) = runtime_elem(inner) {\n318:         depth += 1;\n319:         inner = elem;\n320:     }\n321:     depth\n322: }\n323: \n324: /// One `map`/`select` applied between reading a record and printing it, in source order (the\n325: /// stage nearest the source first).\n326: pub enum Stage<'a> {\n327:     Map { param: LocalId, body: &'a Tir },\n328:     Select { param: LocalId, pred: &'a Tir },\n329: }\n330: \n331: <<<<<<< HEAD\n332: /// What a fused loop reads one entry at a time: a raw line of stdin, to be parsed by whatever\n333: /// `parse` calls sit in the stages.\n334: #[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n335: pub enum Source {\n336:     Stdin,\n337: =======\n338: /// What a fused loop reads one entry at a time: parsed JSON values, raw lines, or integers\n339: /// counted out of a `range` call.\n340: #[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n341: pub enum Source<'a> {\n342:     Inputs,\n343:     Lines,\n344:     /// `range(n)` with its already-checked bound `n`, evaluated once before the loop runs.\n345:     Range(&'a Tir),\n346: >>>>>>> origin/main\n347: }\n348: \n349: /// A stream-typed pipeline ending in `jsonlines`, compiled as a read-one/transform-one/\n350: /// write-one loop. Decided by the types, not by a structural guess: the old `recognize_fusion`\n351: /// pattern match retired when `Stream` entered the type grammar (plans/streams.md step 5), so\n352: /// whether a program streams is now exactly whether its types say so.\n353: pub struct Fusion<'a> {\n354:     pub source: Source<'a>,\n355:     pub stages: Vec<Stage<'a>>,\n356: }\n357: \n358: /// What a stream chain bottoms out at: a source, a function's own parameter, or the local a\n359: /// `|` bound.\n360: enum Base<'a> {\n361: <<<<<<< HEAD\n362:     Stdin,\n363: =======\n364:     Inputs,\n365:     Lines,\n366:     Range(&'a Tir),\n367: >>>>>>> origin/main\n368:     Var(&'a String),\n369:     Local(LocalId),\n370: }\n371: \n372: /// Append the per-element stages of the stream-typed `t`, returning what its chain bottoms out\n373: /// at.\n374: ///\n375: /// Total, not a recognizer: the checker leaves exactly these kinds able to carry a Stream type\n376: /// (sources, bindings, mappers, calls -- a conditional or match yielding one is refused, and\n377: /// projection is normalized to a Map), so the `unreachable!` arms are compiler bugs, never\n378: /// program shapes. That is the invariant this step lands: a stream-typed program that failed\n379: /// to fuse would silently materialize stdin, which is the defect the feature exists to remove.\n380: fn flatten<'a>(t: &'a Tir, program: &'a Program, stages: &mut Vec<Stage<'a>>) -> Base<'a> {\n381:     match &t.kind {\n382: <<<<<<< HEAD\n383:         Kind::Stdin => Base::Stdin,\n384: =======\n385:         Kind::Inputs => Base::Inputs,\n386:         Kind::Lines => Base::Lines,\n387:         // A stream-typed range is its own birth point: the bound is an ordinary value\n388:         // evaluated once, before the loop.\n389:         Kind::Builtin {\n390:             which: Builtin::Range,\n391:             arg,\n392:         } => Base::Range(arg),\n393: >>>>>>> origin/main\n394:         Kind::Var(name) => Base::Var(name),\n395:         Kind::Local(id) => Base::Local(*id),\n396:         Kind::Bind { local, value, body } => {\n397:             let base = flatten(value, program, stages);\n398:             match flatten(body, program, stages) {\n399:                 Base::Local(id) if id == *local => base,\n400:                 _ => unreachable!(\"a piped stream is consumed by the pipe's own body\"),\n401:             }\n402:         }\n403:         Kind::Map {\n404:             source,\n405:             param,\n406:             body,\n407:         } => {\n408:             let base = flatten(source, program, stages);\n409:             stages.push(Stage::Map {\n410:                 param: *param,\n411:                 body,\n412:             });\n413:             base\n414:         }\n415:         Kind::Select {\n416:             source,\n417:             param,\n418:             pred,\n419:         } => {\n\n(Showing lines 260-419 of 646. Use offset=420 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"    /// `parse(s)`, `Str -> T`: parse one line of stdin (a raw `Str`) as a value of the type\n    /// the position expects. The one place a wire value enters the program, so it is checked\n    /// against the wanted type the way a source used to be, and a backend emits it as its own\n    /// per-type JSON parser over the argument string.\n    Parse,\n}\n\npub struct Func {\n    pub name: String,\n    /// `None` for a nullary function.\n    pub param: Option<String>,\n    pub param_ty: Option<Type>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n}\n\npub struct Program {\n    pub funcs: Vec<Func>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n    /// Whether the program reads `stdin`. A stream is read at most once, and the flag is what","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs","text":"    /// `parse(s)`, `Str -> T`: parse one line of stdin (a raw `Str`) as a value of the type\n    /// the position expects. The one place a wire value enters the program, so it is checked\n    /// against the wanted type the way a source used to be, and a backend emits it as its own\n    /// per-type JSON parser over the argument string.\n    Parse,\n}\n\npub struct Func {\n    pub name: String,\n    /// `None` for a nullary function.\n    pub param: Option<String>,\n    pub param_ty: Option<Type>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n}\n\npub struct Program {\n    pub funcs: Vec<Func>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n    /// Whether the program reads `stdin`. A stream is read at most once, and the flag is what\n    /// lets the host connect the real stdin through rather than pre-reading it.\n    pub uses_stdin: bool,\n=======\n    /// The type stdin must have, if the program reads it.\n    pub input: Option<Type>,\n    /// The element type each line of stdin parses as, if the program reads `inputs`.\n    pub inputs: Option<Type>,\n    /// Whether the program reads `lines`. A separate flag from `input`, since the two are\n    /// unrelated readers of the same real stdin and a program using `lines` alone still needs\n    /// it connected, even though `input` is `None`.\n    pub uses_lines: bool,\n    /// The delimiter the program splits raw lines on, if it reads `dsv`/`csv`/`tsv`. It\n    /// reads the same real stdin as the other three, so it is exclusive with them.\n    pub dsv: Option<String>,\n>>>>>>> origin/main\n    /// Every enum the program declared, the prelude's included. A backend has no checker `Ctx`\n    /// to hand, and the variant list on a `Type::Enum` is a placeholder wherever a recursive\n    /// enum's payload reaches back to itself, so this travels with the tree: it is what\n    /// `ty::variants` re-derives from (kantord/toylang#94).\n    pub enums: Enums,\n}\n\n/// The element type a backend iterates over. Under eager lowering a stream is materialized as\n/// the Vec of its entries, so a Stream's element counts exactly as a Vec's here; the checker's\n/// `Type::elem` deliberately does not agree, keeping the reducers Vec-only at the surface.\npub fn runtime_elem(ty: &Type) -> Option<&Type> {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some(t),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\n/// How many dimension layers wrap a scalar -- `Vec`s, plus the outermost `Stream` when there\n/// is one. Field access distributes over each of them.\npub fn vec_depth(ty: &Type) -> usize {\n    let mut depth = 0;\n    let mut inner = ty;\n    while let Some(elem) = runtime_elem(inner) {\n        depth += 1;\n        inner = elem;\n    }\n    depth\n}\n\n/// One `map`/`select` applied between reading a record and printing it, in source order (the\n/// stage nearest the source first).\npub enum Stage<'a> {\n    Map { param: LocalId, body: &'a Tir },\n    Select { param: LocalId, pred: &'a Tir },\n}\n\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n/// What a fused loop reads one entry at a time: a raw line of stdin, to be parsed by whatever\n/// `parse` calls sit in the stages.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Source {\n    Stdin,\n=======\n/// What a fused loop reads one entry at a time: parsed JSON values, raw lines, or integers\n/// counted out of a `range` call.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\npub enum Source<'a> {\n    Inputs,\n    Lines,\n    /// `range(n)` with its already-checked bound `n`, evaluated once before the loop runs.\n    Range(&'a Tir),\n>>>>>>> origin/main\n}\n\n/// A stream-typed pipeline ending in `jsonlines`, compiled as a read-one/transform-one/\n/// write-one loop. Decided by the types, not by a structural guess: the old `recognize_fusion`\n/// pattern match retired when `Stream` entered the type grammar (plans/streams.md step 5), so\n/// whether a program streams is now exactly whether its types say so.\npub struct Fusion<'a> {\n    pub source: Source<'a>,\n    pub stages: Vec<Stage<'a>>,\n}\n\n/// What a stream chain bottoms out at: a source, a function's own parameter, or the local a\n/// `|` bound.\nenum Base<'a> {\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n    Stdin,\n=======\n    Inputs,\n    Lines,\n    Range(&'a Tir),\n>>>>>>> origin/main\n    Var(&'a String),\n    Local(LocalId),\n}\n\n/// Append the per-element stages of the stream-typed `t`, returning what its chain bottoms out\n/// at.\n///\n/// Total, not a recognizer: the checker leaves exactly these kinds able to carry a Stream type\n/// (sources, bindings, mappers, calls -- a conditional or match yielding one is refused, and\n/// projection is normalized to a Map), so the `unreachable!` arms are compiler bugs, never\n/// program shapes. That is the invariant this step lands: a stream-typed program that failed\n/// to fuse would silently materialize stdin, which is the defect the feature exists to remove.\nfn flatten<'a>(t: &'a Tir, program: &'a Program, stages: &mut Vec<Stage<'a>>) -> Base<'a> {\n    match &t.kind {\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n        Kind::Stdin => Base::Stdin,\n=======\n        Kind::Inputs => Base::Inputs,\n        Kind::Lines => Base::Lines,\n        // A stream-typed range is its own birth point: the bound is an ordinary value\n        // evaluated once, before the loop.\n        Kind::Builtin {\n            which: Builtin::Range,\n            arg,\n        } => Base::Range(arg),\n>>>>>>> origin/main\n        Kind::Var(name) => Base::Var(name),\n        Kind::Local(id) => Base::Local(*id),\n        Kind::Bind { local, value, body } => {\n            let base = flatten(value, program, stages);\n            match flatten(body, program, stages) {\n                Base::Local(id) if id == *local => base,\n                _ => unreachable!(\"a piped stream is consumed by the pipe's own body\"),\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Map {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            let base = flatten(source, program, stages);\n            stages.push(Stage::Map {\n                param: *param,\n                body,\n            });\n            base\n        }\n        Kind::Select {\n            source,\n            param,\n            pred,\n        } => {","lineStart":260,"lineEnd":419,"totalLines":646,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"start":1788372476173,"end":1788372476176}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"For the first conflict in tir.rs (lines 61-76): \n\n- HEAD side: `Stdin,` with doc comment \"The one source:the stream of raw lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>`\".\n- origin/main side: `Input, Inputs, Lines, Dsv { delim: String },` with their doc comments.\n\nThe merge policy: take origin/main's content, applying our retirement delta. Our retirement: replace Input/Inputs/Lines with Stdin. What about Dsv? Our branch didn't know Dsv (added later by main.. So Dsv stays. The retired side: Stdin replaces Input/Inputs/Lines; Dsv remains.\n\n.\n\nBut wait -- hold on. If our retirement's intent is \"for one Stream<Str> source\", and origin/main ALSO has `Dsv` (a delimiter-split source that produces Vec<Vec<Str>>... hmm that's a different kind of source -- DSV reading. Does our retirement retire DSV too? The commit message says \"Retire input/inputs/lines for one stdin source\". Only input/inputs/lines. Dsv wasn't in the base at all. So keep Dsv.\n\n.\n\nSo merged tir.rs Kind enum:\n\n```\n    /// The one source: the stream of raw lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>``.\n    Stdin,\n    /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`:the\n    /// parameterized DSV source. `csv`/`tsv` arrive here with the delimiter already fixed.\n    Dsv {\n        delim: String,\n    },\n```\n\nHmm, but which doc comment for Stdin? Origin/main doesn't have Stdin at all. Our branch's doc comment for Stdin: \"The one source: the stream of raw lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>`.\". That's our retirement's version. Keep it. And keep Dsv's doc comment from origin/main.\n\n.\n\nNow, there's a subtlety about the ordering. Origin/main enum order: Input, Inputs, Lines, Dsv. Our retirement replaces Input/Inputs/Lines with a single Stdin. The Dsv position: after Stdin. So merged order: Stdin, Dsv.\n\n. Good, that's what I'll write.\n\n.\n\nLet me now read the rest of tir.rs conflict blocks.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634e610b00138rN5aspkFbTLK","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634e503b001UambQnDpCkhZ0D"}}
