{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372490356,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_64224fc99936447ab53fecf9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs","limit":140,"offset":121},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n121:     Select {\n122:         source: Box<Tir>,\n123:         param: LocalId,\n124:         pred: Box<Tir>,\n125:     },\n126:     /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n127:     /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n128:     Field {\n129:         base: Box<Tir>,\n130:         name: String,\n131:     },\n132:     /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n133:     ///\n134:     /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n135:     /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n136:     /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n137:     /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n138:     Builtin {\n139:         which: Builtin,\n140:         arg: Box<Tir>,\n141:     },\n142:     /// Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down. Like a field access and unlike an index,\n143:     /// the depth is every Vec layer of the base, because an Opt is not a dimension.\n144:     Unwrap {\n145:         base: Box<Tir>,\n146:     },\n147:     Index {\n148:         base: Box<Tir>,\n149:         index: Box<Tir>,\n150:         depth: usize,\n151:         /// Whether an entry is a record, which decides if collapsing has to gather columns.\n152:         elem_is_record: bool,\n153:     },\n154:     /// Narrow a dimension to the `[start, end)` window, `depth` layers down, counting negative\n155:     /// bounds from the end. Out-of-range bounds clamp to the valid range rather than answering\n156:     /// `Opt` the way a collapsing `Index` does (kantord/toylang#143), jq's `.[a:b]`; `None`\n157:     /// means the dimension's own boundary (0 and its length), and `start >= end` after\n158:     /// clamping yields empty.\n159:     Slice {\n160:         base: Box<Tir>,\n161:         start: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n162:         end: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n163:         depth: usize,\n164:     },\n165:     /// First-match-wins dispatch over the subject: variant arms test its shape, guard arms\n166:     /// evaluate a Bool of their own. The checker has already resolved every name a pattern\n167:     /// bound, so an arm is only a test, a payload local to bind, and a body.\n168:     ///\n169:     /// When the chain is total (`partial` is false) -- every variant covered, or a default at\n170:     /// the end -- a backend may take the last arm without a test. A partial chain has no such\n171:     /// arm: every test is emitted, and falling off the end produces the absent `Opt`, which is\n172:     /// why the node's type is then `Opt` of the arms' common body type.\n173:     Match {\n174:         subject: Box<Tir>,\n175:         arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n176:         partial: bool,\n177:     },\n178: }\n179: \n180: pub struct MatchArm {\n181:     /// `Some` tests the subject for this variant. `None` with a `guard` is a guard arm; `None`\n182:     /// with no guard is the default arm, which the checker keeps last.\n183:     pub variant: Option<String>,\n184:     /// The Bool deciding a guard arm, already checked in the subject's scope.\n185:     pub guard: Option<Tir>,\n186:     /// The local the payload binds to in a payload-variant arm; `.` and destructured field\n187:     /// names in the body both read through it.\n188:     pub payload: Option<LocalId>,\n189:     pub body: Tir,\n190: }\n191: \n192: /// The functions the language provides. Each is unary, and so is every user function: something\n193: /// wanting two arguments takes a record, which is what a record literal is for in argument\n194: /// position.\n195: #[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n196: pub enum Builtin {\n197:     /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n198:     IntToStr,\n199:     /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n200:     /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n201:     /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n202:     /// the cast.\n203:     IntToI64,\n204:     /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n205:     /// Python, and this language's own indices.\n206:     Range,\n207:     /// `collect(s)`, `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream. What\n208:     /// comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace left\n209:     /// of how it arrived.\n210:     Collect,\n211:     /// `jsonlines(v)`, printing each element of a `Vec<T>` on its own line rather than wrapping\n212:     /// the whole thing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON):\n213:     /// one JSON value per line. Polymorphic over the element type, so it is not in the fixed\n214:     /// signature table `builtin()` reads from; `synth` checks it directly, the way `map` and\n215:     /// `select` are checked from their own arm rather than through a table.\n216:     JsonLines,\n217:     /// `length(v)`, a Vec's length. A dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so reading it out\n218:     /// costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name.\n219:     Length,\n220:     /// `flatten(vv)`, flattening a `Vec<Vec<T>>` into one `Vec<T>`, for the case where the\n221:     /// number of inner Vecs is not known at the call site. Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs\n222:     /// is `+` instead (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading of Q2 in plans/questions.md); this\n223:     /// builtin used to be named `concat` and cover both cases before that split.\n224:     Flatten,\n225:     /// `tail(v)`, every element but the first, `None` when `v` is empty. Consistent with how\n226:     /// `Index` already turns \"reaching past what's there\" into `Opt` rather than a runtime\n227:     /// failure.\n228:     Tail,\n229:     /// `fields(r)`, a record's field names in declaration order (kantord/toylang#63, the\n230:     /// accessor #60's ratification promised once order left type identity). The names come from\n231:     /// `r`'s checked type, not its runtime value, so every backend can emit them as a literal;\n232:     /// `r` is still evaluated for whatever it would otherwise do, the same as any other argument.\n233:     Fields,\n234:     /// `chars(s)`, `Str -> Vec<Char>`: every Unicode scalar value in `s`, in order. Decoded by\n235:     /// codepoint on every backend, so a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one\n236:     /// element here even on a target whose own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it\n237:     /// (kantord/toylang#75).\n238:     Chars,\n239:     /// `sort(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in ascending order by the same total order\n240:     /// `<` already gives `T`. One value in, one value out with no lawful Stream instance\n241:     /// (kantord/toylang#86, Q20 in plans/questions.md), so it is checked as a Vec-only builtin the way\n242:     /// `length`/`tail`/`flatten` are rather than through `select`/`map`'s cardinality\n243:     /// polymorphism. Restricted to the element types ordering already typechecks on --\n244:     /// Int, Int64, Str, Char -- so every backend can reach for its native ordering.\n245:     Sort,\n246:     /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n247:     /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n248:     /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n249:     Reverse,\n250:     /// `sum(v)`, the reduction of `+` over `v`'s elements, at the element type's own width: a\n251:     /// `Vec<Int>` sums to `Int`, a `Vec<Int64>` to `Int64`, and an empty Vec sums to 0. Each\n252:     /// addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the width is the\n253:     /// same number a hand-written fold would produce (kantord/toylang#140).\n254:     Sum,\n255:     /// `max(v)`, the greatest of `v`'s elements, `Opt<T>` because an empty Vec has no maximum\n256:     /// -- the same answer indexing gives to absence (kantord/toylang#140). Restricted to the\n257:     /// same two integer element types `sum` takes, so a backend can reach for its native\n258:     /// maximum.\n259:     Max,\n260:     /// `parse(s)`, `Str -> T`: parse one line of stdin (a raw `Str`) as a value of the type\n\n(Showing lines 121-260 of 646. Use offset=261 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"    Select {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        pred: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n    /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n    Field {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        name: String,\n    },\n    /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n    ///\n    /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n    /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n    /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n    /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n    Builtin {\n        which: Builtin,\n        arg: Box<Tir>,","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-159/src/tir.rs","text":"    Select {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        pred: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n    /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n    Field {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        name: String,\n    },\n    /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n    ///\n    /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n    /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n    /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n    /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n    Builtin {\n        which: Builtin,\n        arg: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down. Like a field access and unlike an index,\n    /// the depth is every Vec layer of the base, because an Opt is not a dimension.\n    Unwrap {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    Index {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        index: Box<Tir>,\n        depth: usize,\n        /// Whether an entry is a record, which decides if collapsing has to gather columns.\n        elem_is_record: bool,\n    },\n    /// Narrow a dimension to the `[start, end)` window, `depth` layers down, counting negative\n    /// bounds from the end. Out-of-range bounds clamp to the valid range rather than answering\n    /// `Opt` the way a collapsing `Index` does (kantord/toylang#143), jq's `.[a:b]`; `None`\n    /// means the dimension's own boundary (0 and its length), and `start >= end` after\n    /// clamping yields empty.\n    Slice {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        start: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n        end: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n        depth: usize,\n    },\n    /// First-match-wins dispatch over the subject: variant arms test its shape, guard arms\n    /// evaluate a Bool of their own. The checker has already resolved every name a pattern\n    /// bound, so an arm is only a test, a payload local to bind, and a body.\n    ///\n    /// When the chain is total (`partial` is false) -- every variant covered, or a default at\n    /// the end -- a backend may take the last arm without a test. A partial chain has no such\n    /// arm: every test is emitted, and falling off the end produces the absent `Opt`, which is\n    /// why the node's type is then `Opt` of the arms' common body type.\n    Match {\n        subject: Box<Tir>,\n        arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n        partial: bool,\n    },\n}\n\npub struct MatchArm {\n    /// `Some` tests the subject for this variant. `None` with a `guard` is a guard arm; `None`\n    /// with no guard is the default arm, which the checker keeps last.\n    pub variant: Option<String>,\n    /// The Bool deciding a guard arm, already checked in the subject's scope.\n    pub guard: Option<Tir>,\n    /// The local the payload binds to in a payload-variant arm; `.` and destructured field\n    /// names in the body both read through it.\n    pub payload: Option<LocalId>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n}\n\n/// The functions the language provides. Each is unary, and so is every user function: something\n/// wanting two arguments takes a record, which is what a record literal is for in argument\n/// position.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Builtin {\n    /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n    IntToStr,\n    /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n    /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n    /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n    /// the cast.\n    IntToI64,\n    /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n    /// Python, and this language's own indices.\n    Range,\n    /// `collect(s)`, `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream. What\n    /// comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace left\n    /// of how it arrived.\n    Collect,\n    /// `jsonlines(v)`, printing each element of a `Vec<T>` on its own line rather than wrapping\n    /// the whole thing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON):\n    /// one JSON value per line. Polymorphic over the element type, so it is not in the fixed\n    /// signature table `builtin()` reads from; `synth` checks it directly, the way `map` and\n    /// `select` are checked from their own arm rather than through a table.\n    JsonLines,\n    /// `length(v)`, a Vec's length. A dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so reading it out\n    /// costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name.\n    Length,\n    /// `flatten(vv)`, flattening a `Vec<Vec<T>>` into one `Vec<T>`, for the case where the\n    /// number of inner Vecs is not known at the call site. Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs\n    /// is `+` instead (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading of Q2 in plans/questions.md); this\n    /// builtin used to be named `concat` and cover both cases before that split.\n    Flatten,\n    /// `tail(v)`, every element but the first, `None` when `v` is empty. Consistent with how\n    /// `Index` already turns \"reaching past what's there\" into `Opt` rather than a runtime\n    /// failure.\n    Tail,\n    /// `fields(r)`, a record's field names in declaration order (kantord/toylang#63, the\n    /// accessor #60's ratification promised once order left type identity). The names come from\n    /// `r`'s checked type, not its runtime value, so every backend can emit them as a literal;\n    /// `r` is still evaluated for whatever it would otherwise do, the same as any other argument.\n    Fields,\n    /// `chars(s)`, `Str -> Vec<Char>`: every Unicode scalar value in `s`, in order. Decoded by\n    /// codepoint on every backend, so a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one\n    /// element here even on a target whose own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it\n    /// (kantord/toylang#75).\n    Chars,\n    /// `sort(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in ascending order by the same total order\n    /// `<` already gives `T`. One value in, one value out with no lawful Stream instance\n    /// (kantord/toylang#86, Q20 in plans/questions.md), so it is checked as a Vec-only builtin the way\n    /// `length`/`tail`/`flatten` are rather than through `select`/`map`'s cardinality\n    /// polymorphism. Restricted to the element types ordering already typechecks on --\n    /// Int, Int64, Str, Char -- so every backend can reach for its native ordering.\n    Sort,\n    /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n    /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n    /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n    Reverse,\n    /// `sum(v)`, the reduction of `+` over `v`'s elements, at the element type's own width: a\n    /// `Vec<Int>` sums to `Int`, a `Vec<Int64>` to `Int64`, and an empty Vec sums to 0. Each\n    /// addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the width is the\n    /// same number a hand-written fold would produce (kantord/toylang#140).\n    Sum,\n    /// `max(v)`, the greatest of `v`'s elements, `Opt<T>` because an empty Vec has no maximum\n    /// -- the same answer indexing gives to absence (kantord/toylang#140). Restricted to the\n    /// same two integer element types `sum` takes, so a backend can reach for its native\n    /// maximum.\n    Max,\n    /// `parse(s)`, `Str -> T`: parse one line of stdin (a raw `Str`) as a value of the type","lineStart":121,"lineEnd":260,"totalLines":646,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"sta