{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372545049,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_1942b3e952b843daa5345fac","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git diff origin/main -- src/tir.rs | head -80"},"output":"diff --git a/src/tir.rs b/src/tir.rs\nindex ea88d66..100547a 100644\n--- a/src/tir.rs\n+++ b/src/tir.rs\n@@ -1,36 +1,33 @@\n //! The typed IR the backends consume.\n-//!\n+\n //! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n //! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n //! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n-//!\n+\n //! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n //! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n-\n+ \n use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\n use crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n-\n+ \n pub struct Tir {\n     pub ty: Type,\n     pub kind: Kind,\n }\n-\n+ \n impl Tir {\n     pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n         Tir { ty, kind }\n     }\n }\n-\n+ \n /// A binding the source cannot name, introduced for `|` and for `select`'s parameter.\n-pub type LocalId = u32;\n \n+pub type LocalId = u32;\n+ \n pub enum Kind {\n     Str(String),\n     Int(i64),\n-    /// A float literal: an IEEE 754 binary64 double (ADR 0007). Like `Int`, the width lives\n-    /// in the node's own type -- there is only one float width, so a `Float` node is always\n-    /// the binary64 the ADR picks, and no other backend needs to consult the type to know.\n-    Float(f64),\n     VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n     /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n     /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n@@ -39,7 +36,7 @@ pub enum Kind {\n         fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n     },\n     /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n-    /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n+    /// position (its tag,where one is needed)and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n     /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n     /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n     /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n@@ -60,22 +57,20 @@ pub enum Kind {\n         body: Box<Tir>,\n     },\n     /// A name written in the source: today only a function parameter.\n+\n     Var(String),\n     Local(LocalId),\n-    /// The value read from stdin.\n-    Input,\n-    /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, eagerly collected into a `Vec<T>`.\n-    Inputs,\n-    /// The stream of lines read from stdin, read incrementally by whatever consumes it.\n-    Lines,\n-    /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`: the\n+    /// The one source:the stream of raw lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>``.\n+\n+    Stdin,\n+    /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`:the\n     /// parameterized DSV source. `csv`/`tsv` arrive here with the delimiter already fixed.\n     Dsv {\n         delim: String,\n     },\n","metadata":{"output":"diff --git a/src/tir.rs b/src/tir.rs\nindex ea88d66..100547a 100644\n--- a/src/tir.rs\n+++ b/src/tir.rs\n@@ -1,36 +1,33 @@\n //! The typed IR the backends consume.\n-//!\n+\n //! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n //! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n //! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n-//!\n+\n //! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n //! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n-\n+ \n use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\n use crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n-\n+ \n pub struct Tir {\n     pub ty: Type,\n     pub kind: Kind,\n }\n-\n+ \n impl Tir {\n     pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n         Tir { ty, kind }\n     }\n }\n-\n+ \n /// A binding the source cannot name, introduced for `|` and for `select`'s parameter.\n-pub type LocalId = u32;\n \n+pub type LocalId = u32;\n+ \n pub enum Kind {\n     Str(String),\n     Int(i64),\n-    /// A float literal: an IEEE 754 binary64 double (ADR 0007). Like `Int`, the width lives\n-    /// in the node's own type -- there is only one float width, so a `Float` node is always\n-    /// the binary64 the ADR picks, and no other backend needs to consult the type to know.\n-    Float(f64),\n     VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n     /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n     /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n@@ -39,7 +36,7 @@ pub enum Kind {\n         fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n     },\n     /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n-    /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n+    /// position (its tag,where one is needed)and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n     /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n     /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n     /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n@@ -60,22 +57,20 @@ pub enum Kind {\n         body: Box<Tir>,\n     },\n     /// A name written in the source: today only a function parameter.\n+\n     Var(String),\n     Local(LocalId),\n-    /// The value read from stdin.\n-    Input,\n-    /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, eagerly collected into a `Vec<T>`.\n-    Inputs,\n-    /// The stream of lines read from stdin, read incrementally by whatever consumes it.\n-    Lines,\n-    /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`: the\n+    /// The one source:the stream of raw lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>``.\n+\n+    Stdin,\n+    /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`:the\n     /// parameterized DSV source. `csv`/`tsv` arrive here with the delimiter already fixed.\n     Dsv {\n         delim: String,\n     },\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git diff origin/main -- src/tir.rs | head -80","time":{"start":1788372545045,"end":1788372545047}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Hmm wait. I made a big mistake. When I wrote the file, I substituted `,` and `.` and `(`, etc. with odd artifacts. Actually looking at my written content, I corrupted EVERYTHING: I turned `,` into `,`?? No wait. Let me look at what I actually wrote. I typed the file with bizarre extra spaces and odd characters like `=` replaced by `,`. \n\nWait, I need to check: did I write the file correctly? Let me re-read what I wrote. Looking at my Write content above... I wrote things like \"pub struct Tir { ... pub ty: Type,\" -- those look fine. But then \"/// What a fused loop reads one entry at a time: a raw line of stdin,,to be parsed\" -- with double commas?? Hmm. \n\nActually wait, I need to look at my actual written content carefully. I believe I introduced corruption: I wrote `ty: Type,` correctly in some places but in the doc comments I typed `,,` (double comma) and `,.` etc. This is a mess -- I clearly made transcription errors while retyping. E.g.:\n\n- \"/// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\" -- wait in my written version I have \"/// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\" hmm.\n\n Actually my content reads: \"    /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\" -- hmm that looks OK.\n\n \n\nHold on. Let me actually diff my written file against origin/main to see the damage.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634f6e0a001iuZBHLBcsjQLCJ","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634f5e57001vsao14E9IIMd91"}}
