{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372568342,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_03b2d4e1b5d54f079a2635ff","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show 7c4cc86:src/check/mod.rs | grep -n 'input\\|Input\\|lines\\|Lines\\|stdin' | head -40"},"output":"31:    /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n32:    input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n33:    /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n34:    /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n35:    inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n36:    /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n39:    lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n41:    /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n42:    /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n45:    /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n60:            input: self.input,\n61:            inputs: self.inputs,\n62:            lines_used: self.lines_used,\n77:    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n113:    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n114:    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n123:        input: &input,\n124:        inputs: &inputs,\n125:        lines_used: &lines_used,\n157:    // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n165:    let input = input.into_inner();\n166:    let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n167:    // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n169:    // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n170:    // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n171:    // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n172:    if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n175:            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n180:    if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n183:            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n188:    if lines_used.get() && inputs.is_some() {\n191:            \"a program cannot use both `lines` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n199:        input,\n200:        inputs,\n201:        uses_lines: lines_used.get(),\n231:            input: ctx.input,\n232:            inputs: ctx.inputs,\n233:            lines_used: ctx.lines_used,\n239:        // position (`[]`, `input`, a variant-naming string) resolves against the annotation. A\n294:    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n","metadata":{"output":"31:    /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n32:    input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n33:    /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n34:    /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n35:    inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n36:    /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n39:    lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n41:    /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n42:    /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n45:    /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n60:            input: self.input,\n61:            inputs: self.inputs,\n62:            lines_used: self.lines_used,\n77:    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n113:    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n114:    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n123:        input: &input,\n124:        inputs: &inputs,\n125:        lines_used: &lines_used,\n157:    // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n165:    let input = input.into_inner();\n166:    let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n167:    // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n169:    // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n170:    // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n171:    // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n172:    if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n175:            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n180:    if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n183:            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n188:    if lines_used.get() && inputs.is_some() {\n191:            \"a program cannot use both `lines` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n199:        input,\n200:        inputs,\n201:        uses_lines: lines_used.get(),\n231:            input: ctx.input,\n232:            inputs: ctx.inputs,\n233:            lines_used: ctx.lines_used,\n239:        // position (`[]`, `input`, a variant-naming string) resolves against the annotation. A\n294:    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show 7c4cc86:src/check/mod.rs | grep -n 'input\\|Input\\|lines\\|Lines\\|stdin' | head -40","time":{"start":1788372568337,"end":1788372568340}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Ugh, I corrupted the file badly. I removed the blank lines (the `-//!` and `-` removals),and I typed `= 0;` as `= 0;`?? Let me see... The diff shows:\n\n- `-//!` (blank doc line) removed -- I dropped blank lines between doc-comment sections and code.\n- `-pub type LocalId = u32;` then blank + re-add -- I changed the spacing.\n- `-    /// position (its tag, where one is needed)and its payload type...` → `+    /// position (its tag,where one is needed)and ...` -- I removed spaces after commas in doc comments!!\n\nI clearly have been systematically corrupted the file by retyping (my written content dropped spaces after commas in doc comments and removed blank lines). This is terrible. \n\nThe root problem: I hand-retyped a 646-line file from memory of the reads, and introduced dozens of subtle corruptions. This is exactly the failure mode the brief warned about but worse. \n\nI need a better approach. Instead of Write-whole-file for these big files, I should use the `edit` tool?? The brief says DO NOT use Edit for conflict resolution because exact-string matching kept failing. But the failures were due to \"subtle whitespace/encoding mismatches inside the markers\". Hmm.\n\n. But using Edit on the SMALL marker blocks might still work if I match exactly... but the brief explicitly says don't.\n\n\n\nAlternatively, I can use bash to do the file transformation with a small script, since bash file manipulation is denied (\"bash file writes (> and >> redirection)... always denied\")? Actually the brief says: \"known denials: ... bash file writes (> and >> redirection...\" -- so I can't use bash redirection to write files. And \"CREATE AND EDIT FILES ONLY WITH YOUR write/edit TOOLS\".\n\nHmm. But I need to fix my corrupted tir.rs first, then proceed. \n\nLet me think about the best strategy given the constraints:\n\n1. Write tool requires full file content → error-prone for large files. I corrupted it.\n2. Edit tool does exact string replacement -- can work if I target unique strings. But the brief forbade it for conflict resolution. \n\nHowever, maybe the reason Edit failed before was subtle mismatches in conflict marker content. But I could use Edit to REVERT my corrupted file back... no, that's still editing.\n\n.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. Perhaps a viable approach: for each conflicted file, resolve the conflicts by using the edit tool on the *marker blocks* precisely (which are ASCII-safe and not that subtle...) But the brief explicitly says DO NOT use Edit for conflict resolution. Given the two prior runs failed with Edit, and my Write attempt corrupted a file, maybe the right call is to carefully use Write but with a much more careful process: read the file in full (I did), write it in full, and then verify with a diff against the intended result, fixing discrepancies iteratively (using Write again, since it's the allowed tool).\n\nBut retyping 646 lines from scratch is error-prone. However, I could reconstruct from the ACTUAL file content more reliably: instead of reading the working-tree file (which has markers)and retyping, I should read origin/main's version and HEAD's version and produce the merged file as origin/main's content plus our delta. But that's still retyping.\n\n.\n\nAlternative: use `git show origin/main:src/tir.rs > file`? That's bash redirection → denied. \n\nHmm, what about using the `edit` tool to surgically remove conflict markers? The failures before were about marker content mismatches. But maybe I can make Edit work by using the Read output as the exact source (copy the exact strings from the Read output). The Read output gave me the exact bytes (it displayed them.. Though there was that encoding weirdness in the read output (those odd `;` characters), hmm.\n\nWait -- actually, let me look again at the read output. In my first read of tir.rs, lines looked normal: \"The type `input` was checked against\" etc. But in the write I did, I corrupted things. That's because I retyped rather than copied exactly. \n\nThe real fix: I need to restore tir.rs to the state it was in BEFORE my write (the prior working-tree state with markers),and then handle the differently.\n\n \n\nCan I restore tir.rs? git has it in the index? The working tree file is what I overwrote. The index version (stage 2/3) still holds the merge states. I can restore from the index: `git checkout -- src/tir.rs`? That restores the working-tree file from the index (which during a merge holds the pre-merge... actually during merge, the index holds stages. `git checkout -- file` during a merge takes... hmm. Actually `git checkout -- <file>` replaces working tree with the index version. During a conflicted merge, the index for a conflicted file... does it have a single version? Git keeps stage 1/2/3 in the index. `git checkout -- file` would try to restore a single index entry, which doesn't exist cleanly for a conflicted file. Hmm, it might error.\n\n.\n\nAlternatively, I can reconstruct the pre-write working-tree file from my knowledge: I have all the read outputs of the pre-write file. So I can re-write it correctly, by being VERY careful to copy exactly from the read output (not retype from memory). The read output lines are exact. So I'll rewrite tir.rs again, this time copying character-for-character from the read outputs I have. I have:\n- Lines  ǅ1-120 (first read)\n- Lines  ǅ121-260 (read offset 121\n- Lines  ǅ260-419 (read offset 260\n- Lines  ǅ420-455 (read offset 420\n- Lines  ǅ455-584 (read offset 455\n- Lines  ǅ584-646 (read offset 584\n\nThese reads contain the markers. I need to merge the marker blocks per my plan, keeping everything else byte-identical.\n\nThis time I'll be extremely careful: copy the text exactly as shown in the reads, including blank lines, spaces, everything.\n\nThe key mistakes I made last time: \n- Removed blank lines (the file has blank lines between sections -- e.g. after the `//!` block there's a blank line before `use`; between `}` and `pub struct` etc). I collapsed them.\n- Removed spaces after commas in doc comments?? Actually wait, in the actual file, are there spaces after commas? Looking at the read output: \"/// A record literal, its fields in declaration order\" -- yes, spaces after commas. In my write I had \"/// A record literal, its fields...\" same. Hmm, but the diff showed `-    /// position (its tag, where one is needed)and` → `+    /// position (its tag,where one is needed)and` -- so my written version LOST the space after `tag,`. Because I retyped.\n\nThe other issue: the `;` characters. Looking at the diff output line \"`/// position (its tag, where one is needed)and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\" -- this is the ORIGINAL file. It uses a normal space. Good.\n\nSo this time, I will copy EXACTLY from the Read outputs. Let me carefully redo tir.rs. Actually, this is very painstaking. \n\nHmm, wait. Maybe there's a much better approach I'm missing. What about using `git checkout` with a specific stage to restore,then applying the write only around markers via... no.\n\nWhat about `git merge-file`? No, that needs files and output redirection.\n\n.\n\nActually -- key realization: I could use the `edit` tool after all, but the brief forbade it. However, maybe I can use edit tool in a way that's robust: use it to replace each marker block with its resolution, matching the exact marker text INCLUDING surrounding context. The prior failures were \"subtle whitespace/encoding mismatches inside the markers\". But I've now SEEN the markers in the read outputs. I can copy them exactly. But the risk: if there's an invisible encoding issue, Edit fails and I burn budget. \n\nAlternatively: keep using Write, but verify each file with `git diff origin/main` after writing, and fix any unintended diffs by re-Writing (or by careful Edit).. The diff I just ran showed my corruption. I'll now rewrite tir.rs correctly.\n\n.\n\nGiven the difficulty, let me reconsider the scale. There are 11 files to do. Files like lib.rs are huge (700+ lines?). Hand-writing each is extremely error-prone. \n\nIs there any other allowed way to create a file? \"CREATE AND EDIT FILES ONLY WITH YOUR write/edit TOOLS\". So no cp/git checkout to filesystem... Hmm, wait: is `git checkout <stage> -- <file>` a \"bash file write\"? It's a git operation that writes the working tree file. The brief said \"If a command is denied, adapt with an allowed alternative rather than retrying.\" It didn't enumerate git checkout as denied. And the hard constraint is \"work ONLY inside this worktree\". Using git to restore file content from the index seems legit and not destructive.\n\n. \n\nActually -- here's a thought: for each conflicted file, I could:\n1. `git checkout --theirs -- <file>`?? That would take origin/main's version entirely, losing our retirement. Not right.\n\n2. `git checkout --ours -- <file>`?? That takes HEAD's version, losing origin/main's changes. Not right either.\n\n\n\nHmm, neither is right because we need a merge of both.\n\n\n\nWhat about this: the merge base approach. I can compute the merged file as: origin/main's version + our delta re-applied. Our delta for each file is exactly commit 45c76be's per-file diff. I can:\n1. Get origin/main's version of the file into the working tree: `git checkout origin/main -- <file>`. \n2. Then apply our delta to it using the edit/write tools.\n\n. \n\nBut applying the delta still requires editing. However, our delta per file is SMALL (e.g. parse.rs delta = 4 substitutions; tir.rs delta = a handful of hunks), whereas origin/main's full file is huge. So the strategy: \n\n- For each file: `git checkout origin/main -- <file>` (allowed? it's a git command writing working tree file from the repo, not a shell redirection. I think that's fine,it doesn't use denied constructs. It also stages the file (marks resolved? Actually `git checkout <tree> -- <path>` updates the index and working tree to that tree's version, clearing the merge conflict entry for that path. That effectively resolves the file as \"theirs\".). \n\nThen apply our retirement delta onto it using Write (or Edit? -- our delta edits are small, e.g. renaming Input→Stdin in a few spots, replacing the input/inputs/lines handling with stdin handling).. \n\nBut WAIT: our delta was computed against the BASE, not against origin/main. Origin/main has diverged: e.g. origin/main's check/mod.rs has lots of new code that references input/inputs/lines (e.g. in the \"input <type> annotation\" code from the parse.rs diff, and `expect` etc). Our delta needs to be RE-INTERPRETED in the new context, not blindly applied. E.g. origin/main has an `input` annotation feature (`input <type>` declares what stdin holds)that our branch's retirement removed (the checker's Ctx fields input/inputs were removed). Hmm wait -- actually, hold on. \n\nLet me reconsider what our retirement actually does to check/mod.rs. Our delta: removed `input: RefCell<Option<Type>>`, `inputs: RefCell<Option<Type>>`, `lines_used`→`stdin_used`, removed the mutual-exclusion checks, removed `input_read`/`inputs_read` functions and replaced with `parse_read`, added `parse` builtin name, replaced `Expr::Lines` synth with `Expr::Stdin`, added the \"parse needs a position\" check, removed Expr::Input/Inputs synth arms... \n\nBut origin/main ALSO evolved check/mod.rs a lot after the base: it added the `input <type>` annotation feature (which in the base era existed? Wait -- the working-tree parse.rs diff showed a block \"`input <type>` declares what stdin holds before the body reads it. It is the one declaration that sits after the defs\" -- hmm, this comment was added BY THE PRIOR AGENT? Or is it origin/main's? Let me check: in the `git diff origin/main -- src/parse.rs` output earlier, was that \"input <type> declares\" block present in origin/main? Let me search origin/main's parse.rs for \"declares what stdin holds\".\n\nActually, from the diff of working-tree vs origin/main for parse.rs, the \"input <type> declares what stdin holds\" block was NOT in the diff output I saw (the diff hunks I read covered the token enum, display, read_tok, read_number, tail_pipe, operand, argument_starts, atom)... The \"input <type> declares\" block appeared in the `git diff HEAD -- src/parse.rs` output as a `+` addition -- meaning the working tree ADDED it relative to HEAD. So it's either origin/main's or prior-agent-invented. Since it wasn't in the origin/main-vs-worktree diff (it didn't show as removed), it's IN origin/main too. Wait, that means origin/main HAS the \"input <type>\" annotation feature?? But that conflicts with our retirement...\n\nHmm, this is getting really tangled. Let me directly check origin/main's check/mod.rs for the annotation feature and for input/inputs/lines handling. And our HEAD's check/mod.rs for the same. And then decide the merged check/mod.rs properly. \n\nActually, the fundamental question is: does origin/main still support the `input <type>` annotation (reading stdin as a typed value),which our retirement REPLACED with `parse(.)`? If so, merging means: remove the annotation machinery from origin/main, replacing with stdin+parse.\n\n. That's what our delta does,re-interpreted on origin/main's newer context. This is complex but doable.\n\n.\n\nLet me look at HEAD's check/mod.rs and origin/main's check/mod.rs in full to understand the relationship. Actually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe origin/main's \"input annotation\" feature is NEWER than our base, and our retirement is a REDESIGN of exactly that feature. Let me check the base's check/mod.rs for \"input\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0634fc8f7001WSq8vKry0tobJz","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634f6e3f001x3hpYm56d0cdt1"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372568342,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_a12cb703222f49aa88844213","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show origin/main:src/check/mod.rs | grep -n 'input\\|Input\\|lines\\|Lines\\|stdin' | head -50"},"output":"37:    /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n38:    input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n39:    /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n40:    /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n41:    inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n42:    /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n45:    lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n48:    /// is refused the same way a second `lines` is.\n51:    /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n52:    /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n55:    /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n85:            input: self.input,\n86:            inputs: self.inputs,\n87:            lines_used: self.lines_used,\n105:    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n153:    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n154:    // A program's `input <type>` declaration types stdin up front, before the body is read,\n155:    // instead of borrowing the type from the first use of `input`. Resolving it into the same\n157:    // wire rules `input_read` applies to a use are applied to a declared type here too: a\n159:    if let Some(declared) = &file.input {\n168:                format!(\"`input` cannot be declared as {ty}; it has no wire form to read\"),\n171:        *input.borrow_mut() = Some(ty);\n173:    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n188:        input: &input,\n189:        inputs: &inputs,\n190:        lines_used: &lines_used,\n225:    // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n233:    let input = input.into_inner();\n234:    let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n235:    // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n237:    // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n238:    // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n239:    // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n240:    if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n243:            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n248:    if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n251:            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n256:    if lines_used.get() && inputs.is_some() {\n259:            \"a program cannot use both `lines` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n264:    // `dsv` reads the same raw lines `lines` does, so it joins the same exclusivity: one real\n265:    // stdin, read one way.\n268:        (input.is_some(), \"`input`\"),\n269:        (inputs.is_some(), \"`inputs`\"),\n270:        (lines_used.get(), \"`lines`\"),\n276:                    \"a program cannot use both `dsv` and {name}; they read the same real stdin \\\n285:        input,\n286:        inputs,\n287:        uses_lines: lines_used.get(),\n318:            input: ctx.input,\n319:            inputs: ctx.inputs,\n","metadata":{"output":"37:    /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n38:    input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n39:    /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n40:    /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n41:    inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n42:    /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n45:    lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n48:    /// is refused the same way a second `lines` is.\n51:    /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n52:    /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n55:    /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n85:            input: self.input,\n86:            inputs: self.inputs,\n87:            lines_used: self.lines_used,\n105:    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n153:    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n154:    // A program's `input <type>` declaration types stdin up front, before the body is read,\n155:    // instead of borrowing the type from the first use of `input`. Resolving it into the same\n157:    // wire rules `input_read` applies to a use are applied to a declared type here too: a\n159:    if let Some(declared) = &file.input {\n168:                format!(\"`input` cannot be declared as {ty}; it has no wire form to read\"),\n171:        *input.borrow_mut() = Some(ty);\n173:    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n188:        input: &input,\n189:        inputs: &inputs,\n190:        lines_used: &lines_used,\n225:    // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n233:    let input = input.into_inner();\n234:    let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n235:    // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n237:    // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n238:    // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n239:    // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n240:    if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n243:            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n248:    if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n251: