{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372596880,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_ea94e83b701e450b88bc3560","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show HEAD:src/tir.rs | wc -l"},"output":"577\n","metadata":{"output":"577\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show HEAD:src/tir.rs | wc -l","time":{"start":1788372596874,"end":1788372596877}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So:\n- Base: no `File.input` annotation (no \"input <type>\" feature in ast.rs at base). \n- origin/main: ADDED `File.input: Option<TypeExpr>` (the annotation feature), plus Dsv Expr variant (line  ǅ309 \"Stdin read as raw lines...delimiter...born Vec<Vec<Str>>\"). \n- HEAD: our branch: Stdin only, no annotation field.\n\n.\n\nSo the merged ast.rs should = origin/main's ast.rs, minus the annotation feature (since our retirement kills `input` keyword)?? OR keep the annotation but rename to `stdin <type>`?? \n\nHmm. The retirement says \"three keywords become a single raw Stream<Str> source named `stdin`,and reading a value is an ordinary `parse(.)` call over it\". So the annotation feature (`input <type>` declares the type to read) is rendered obsolete by parse(.) (where the position's expected type resolves the parse call). Our branch removed the whole annotation feature in check/mod.rs (the commit diff shows `input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>` removed, `input_read` removed, Expr::Input synth removed, and the `input <type>` parse-side... well, let me check whether our commit removed the annotation parsing in parse.rs). \n\nOur commit's parse.rs diff (shown earlier) only replaced Input/Inputs/Lines with Stdin in four spots. It did NOT remove an annotation feature. Because the base parse.rs had NO annotation feature (the base's File had no input field; \"input <type>\" wasn't a thing yet). The annotation feature was ADDED by origin/main AFTER the base. So our retirement never saw it.\n\n.\n\nSo in the merge, what do we do with origin/main's annotation feature? Two options:\nA. Drop it entirely (consistent with the retirement's direction: stdin has no declared type; parse(.) infers it from position).and src/parse.rs, src/ast.rs, src/check/mod.rs remove it.\nB. Keep it, adapting to `stdin <type>`.\n\n\n\nHmm. The brief says: \"your branch retired the stdin keywords Input/Inputs/Lines/Dsv for one Stream<Str> source with parse(.) as a library call -- keep that intent, integrate whatever unrelated changes origin/main made to the same file\".\n\nSo \"keep that intent\": the retirement's intent is that stdin is ONE raw source and parsing happens via parse(.); there is no declared-type annotation for stdin. The annotation feature is \"unrelated change origin/main made\" that conflicts with the retirement intent. Since it's directly about `input`, it's NOT unrelated -- it's part of the same feature area that our branch redesigned. So our branch's intent wins: drop the annotation feature.\n\n..\n\nActually wait, hmm. Let me reconsider. Could keeping the annotation feature as `stdin <type>` be the \"integrate unrelated changes\" path? The annotation feature was built on `input` (typed stdin reading). our retirement replaced typed stdin reading with parse(.). The annotation `input <type>` IS the old way of typing stdin, exactly what parse(.) replaces. So no, it should be dropped. The tests/corpus will tell us: tests/corpus/input_annotation.yaml and let_multi_input_annotation.yaml exist in the merged tree (from origin/main, in the \"Changes to be committed\" list). Hmm! Those corpus files spell the old `input` keyword. The brief said the corpus and test files still spell the old keywords and land in a follow-up (\"Source migration only; the corpus and test files still spell the old keywords and land in a follow-up.\"). So tests still use old keywords in the merged world... and they must FAIL?! \n\nWait, that can't be. If the corpus files still spell `input`, and the compiler no longer has an `input` keyword, the corpus cases would fail to parse. But our commit message says \"Source migration only; the corpus and test files still spell the old keywords and land in a follow-up.\" -- meaning our commit did NOT migrate the tests; they were to be migrated in a follow-up. So after our commit, the tests were broken?? That seems odd for a committed state... but maybe that's exactly the state: our branch's commit deliberately left the tests spelling old keywords, and the follow-up migrates them. In the merged world (origin/main + our retirement),the same applies: tests still spell old keywords and would fail until a follow-up migrates them. Hmm, but then `just test` would fail, which contradicts the task's \"verify just check is green\"...\n\nHmm wait. Actually let me re-read the task: \"Then verify `just check` is green\". It says \"just check\", not \"just test\". Let me check what `just check` does. AGENTS.md says \"Run `just test` to run the test suite\". And \"`just` alone lists other recipes\". So `just check` might be a different recipe (lint/compile check, not full tests). Let me check Justfile later. \n\nAlso, maybe the tests in the merged tree DO spell the new keywords because origin/main's tests were already migrated?? The \"Changes to be committed\" list includes modified tests/corpus/input_annotation.yaml etc -- but those are origin/main's versions. Hmm, origin/main HAS `input_annotation.yaml` (since origin/main added the annotation feature with the `input` keyword).. So in the merged world, we keep origin/main's tests (which use `input`/`lines`/etc)but our retirement removes those keywords from the language... → those tests break.\n\n\n\nUnless... hmm. Wait, maybe I'm wrong about the retirement intent for tests. Let me re-read our commit message: \"Source migration only; the corpus and test files still spell the old keywords and land in a follow-up.\" Yes -- so our commit leaves the corpus/test files spelling the old keywords. So they're broken in our branch's committed state?? That can't pass CI...\n\nUnless the test corpus in our branch's state... hmm. Actually maybe the tests DO still pass because the corpus files that used `input`/`inputs`/`lines` are... no, if the keyword is gone from the lexer, `input` parses as an identifier, causing different errors, so `refuses: true` cases might still pass?? But cases with expected `output` would fail.\n\n.\n\nThis is getting too deep. Let me step back and look at the practical task:\n\nThe immediate task: complete the merge correctly, commit, and make `just check` green. The tests might legitimately still fail on old-keyword corpus (and that's a follow-up). But \"just check\" presumably doesn't run the full corpus tests... Actually let me just look at the Justfile.\n\n.\n\nAlso let me reconsider: maybe the intended merged state for tests/corpus is to KEEP origin/main's tests (which use old keywords), and the retirement ALSO kept old keywords... no.\n\nHmm, actually maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me re-examine: did our retirement change the tests? No (commit only touched src/, build.rs, runtime/, main.rs).. And origin/main's tests use old keywords (input/inputs/lines).and dsv/csv/tsv). In the merged world, our retirement removes input/inputs/lines keywords. So merged tests using those keywords break. UNLESS the corpus migration is part of THIS merge task... but the brief says nothing about migrating corpus.\n\n.\"confirm the original task (retire stdin keywords for Stream<Str> + parse(.)) is still intact per gh:159\" -- that's about the source code intent, not tests. And \"verify just check is green\" -- just check, not just test. So maybe the test corpus is allowed to be stale (follow-up),and `just check` is the compile/lint gate that must pass. I'll go with that.\n\nOK, let me refocus. My immediate mess: I corrupted tir.rs. Let me restore it. The cleanest way: `git checkout --theirs -- src/tir.rs`?? No -- I want the PRE-corruption working tree (with markers),which I can reconstruct... Actually, simpler: I can restore the pre-write working-tree tir.rs from the index via `git checkout -- src/tir.rs`... During a merge, `git checkout -- <path>` restores from the index stage... hmm, actually for an unmerged path with multiple stages, `git checkout -- <path>` fails (\"path ... is unmerged\"). Let me try `git checkout -m -- src/tir.rs`? no. \n\nLet me think. The index for unmerged paths stores stages 1/2/3. I want stage  ǅ2 (ours, HEAD).. `git checkout --ours -- src/tir.rs`? -- that writes HEAD's version into working tree AND index (resolving the conflict as ours). That loses origin/main's auto-merged non-conflict content for tir.rs... but wait, I can then redo properly. Hmm, but actually `--ours` for a merge gives our side's file (HEAD version),not the merged-with-nonconflicts working tree. The prior working-tree file had origin/main's non-conflict changes merged in already (that's why it was ~646 lines while HEAD's tir.rs is... let me check HEAD's tir.rs line count). \n\nActually, since the pre-write working-tree tir.rs I have FULLY captured in my read outputs ( and the only changes needed are the 7 marker blocks,, I can just carefully re-Write the file copying from the read outputs exactly. The corruption happened because I retyped lazily. This time I'll copy exactly.\n\n.\n\nBut given the risk, maybe a smarter approach for the remaining 10 files: use `git checkout origin/main -- <file>` to get origin/main's version, then apply our delta via Write... but that still requires writing the whole file if I use Write. Hmm.\n\n.\n\n \n\nWait, actually, is there any way to use Write for a partial update?? No. \n\nWhat about the edit tool? The brief says don't use Edit for conflict resolution. But I've now restored clarity on the content. Perhaps I should just NOT resolve via markers: instead, for each file, I construct the FULL merged file carefully (as origin/main + delta)and Write it. The risk is transcription errors, but I can VERIFY each file after writing with `git diff origin/main -- <file>` and check the diff contains ONLY our intended delta (no whitespace noise). Then fix any noise with targeted Edit calls (which for NON-marker content should be safe -- the failures were with marker-matching specifically... hmm, but the instruction says \"DO NOT use Edit for conflict resolution\". Fixing transcription noise isn't \"conflict resolution\" per se, but it's still editing...). \n\nAlternatively I can use Write again for fixes (whole-file rewrite each time,, painful but safe...but also the diff-based verification catches issues)..\n\nOK here's my refined plan:\n1. For each of the 11 files: \n   a. Read the full working-tree file (with markers)in chunks.\n   b. Construct the merged content in my head (copying the non-conflict parts exactly from the reads, resolving marker blocks per policy).\n   c. Write the file.\n   d. Run `git diff origin/main -- <file>` and verify the only differences are our intended retirement delta. Fix any noise with a follow-up Write (or Edit).\n2. For the 4 resolved-but-unstaged files (build.rs, ast.rs, check/linearity.rs, tags.rs)and parse.rs (once its marker removed):`git add` them.\n3. Commit the merge, then just check.\n\n\nBut the transcription risk is real. For big files like lib.rs, this will be very painful and error-prone. \n\nHmm, is there a way to make Write less error-prone? I could generate the merged file content by... no, I can't run scripts that write files. \n\nWait -- actually, let me reconsider the \"edit tool\" prohibition once more. The brief's root-cause analysis: \"trying to resolve conflict-marker blocks with the Edit tool's exact-string matching, which kept failing on subtle whitespace/encoding mismatches inside the markers\". So Edit failed ON THE MARKERS. Maybe using Edit on NON-marker content works fine. But resolving markers requires matching marker text... which fails. \n\nSo the plan could be: use Edit to modify the non-marker parts... no, the markers are what need changing. \n\nAlternatively: use Edit with SHORT unique anchor strings that avoid the problematic whitespace regions. E.g. for tir.rs block 1: I want to replace:\n\n```\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n    /// The one source:the stream of raw lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>``.\n    Stdin,\n=======\n    /// The value read from stdin.\n\n    Input,\n    ...\n=======\n```\nwith...\n\nBut Edit needs the FULL oldString. If I match the whole marker block, I risk mismatches. If I match just the marker lines themselves (e.g. `<<<<<<< HEAD`), that's not unique enough...\n\nHmm. Actually the failure might have been due to the specific weird chars (like the odd `;`/non-breaking spaces?) in the file. Let me examine the actual bytes of a marker block in tir.rs to see if there's something weird (e.g. non-ASCII whitespace).. Let me hexdump a small region.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063503870001jGJwY4Bz0AKvQ8","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0634fdb35001ZQdo6a577dFzav"}}
