{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788372605536,"sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_5792e78126f14e4784346c4c","state":{"status":"error","input":{"command":"git show :2:src/check/mod.rs | sed -n '1,5p' | od -c | head -20"},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788372605507,"end":1788372605534}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait, `git diff --stat` now shows tir.rs with \"453 ++++++++++++...\" changes -- that's because my corrupt write made a huge diff vs the index stage 2/3. Let me check: the diff stat shows \"Unmerged\" lines for all 16 -- because they're unmerged paths, the diff --stat shows the diff vs... hmm, actually for unmerged paths, `git diff` compares working tree vs stage 1/2/3 combined (showing conflicts). The \"453 +++\" for tir.rs indicates my write changed a LOT vs the merge stages.\n\n.\n\nI need to restore tir.rs to the pre-corruption state. Let me use `git checkout -m -- src/tir.rs`?? Actually, the standard way to restore a conflicted file to the auto-merge state (with conflict markers,is:\n\n```\ngit checkout -m -- <path>\n```\n\nNo wait. `git checkout -m` is for \"merge\"ing when switching branches with local changes. Not that.\n\n\n\nFor unmerged paths, I can restore the working-tree file from the index stages using:\n\n```\ngit checkout --ours -- <path>   # restore HEAD version\ngit checkout --theirs -- <path>  # restore origin/main version\ngit checkout --merge -- <path>   # ?? \n```\n\nHmm. There's also:\n\n```\ngit restore --staged --worktree ... \n```\n\nActually, the simplest reliable way to re-create the conflicted working-tree file: run `git merge --abort`?? No, that aborts everything.\n\n.\n\n\n\nOK here's the thing: I have FULL read outputs of the pre-corruption tir.rs (lines 1-646, complete!). I can rewrite it exactly, resolving markers. I'll be disciplined this time. Since I have the exact text in the read outputs, I'll copy it verbatim, changing only the 7 conflict blocks.\n\n \n\nActually -- wait. Instead of me hand-copying the giant file, could I use the bash `git show` with a pipe to strip markers... no, that's scripty and denied (heredocs/redirection)..\n\n \n\nHmm, what about `git apply`? I could craft a patch that transforms origin/main's tir.rs into the merged version, and `git apply` it (git apply modifies files in place, no redirection, no loops... is it denied?? The brief's denial list: \"gh issue list/search, rm, environment-variable-prefixed commands, shell loop constructs, heredocs, bash file writes (> and >>), writes under /tmp, direct binary execution, multi-command bash lines\". `git apply` isn't in the list. But crafting a patch is essentially writing a file... which I'd do via Write tool (allowed),then `git apply patch` via bash (maybe allowed?). The patch would be a unified diff from origin/main's version to the desired merged version. I'd need to produce that diff content... which requires knowing the exact merged content -- same transcription problem, but patch format is more compact (only the changed hunks).. And applying via git is reliable (no whitespace issues beyond the patch itself,which I control).\n\nBut writing a patch by hand is also error-prone, though less so than the whole file. Hmm.\n\n.\n\n \n\nActually, let me reconsider the Edit-tool path more seriously. The prior agents' Edit failures were on the marker blocks, possibly due to invisible characters (e.g. non-breaking spaces? UTF-8 BOM? CRLF?). Let me hexdump the marker region of the CURRENT tir.rs... but I overwrote it. Let me instead check the marker region bytes in ANOTHER conflicted file that I haven't touched (e.g. src/check/mod.rs) to see if there's something unusual in the file encoding that breaks Edit matching.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063505a17001t4STorp22GSwxx","sessionID":"ses_f9cb37b32ffekf1PK55ZywltbG","messageID":"msg_0635038b0001b4G065uzBu5Ecd"}}
